\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Profits, Scale Economies, and 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash

\backslash

\end_layout

\end_inset

 the Gains from Trade and Industrial Policy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
This paper examines the efficacy of second-best trade restrictions at correcting
 misallocation in the domestic economy.
 To this end, we derive sufficient statistics formulas for first-best and
 second-best optimal policies in an important class of quantitative trade
 models where misallocation stems from scale economies or profit-generating
 markups.
 We then estimate the essential parameters of the model and apply our optimal
 policy formulas to quantify the ex-ante gains from trade and industrial
 policy among major countries.
 Our estimates reveal 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 that standalone trade policy measures are remarkably ineffective at correcting
 misallocation; 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

unilateral adoption of corrective industrial policies is also ineffective
 as it leads to immiserizing growth; but 
\begin_inset Formula $(iii)$
\end_inset

 industrial policies coordinated internationally via deep agreements are
 more transformative than any unilateral policy alternative.
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Old abstract:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
Trade restrictions are often used as 
\begin_inset Formula $(a)$
\end_inset

 a first-best policy for terms-of-trade manipulation or 
\begin_inset Formula $(b)$
\end_inset

 a second-best policy for correcting misallocation in the domestic economy.
 This paper examines the (in)effectiveness of trade restrictions in achieving
 these goals.
 To this end, we derive sufficient statistics formulas for 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
second-best
\emph default
 optimal policies in an important class of quantitative trade models where
 misallocation stems from scale economies or profit-generating markups.
 We then estimate the essential parameters of the model and apply our optimal
 policy formulas to quantify the ex-ante gains from trade and industrial
 policy among major countries.
 Our estimates reveal that 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 standalone trade policy measures are remarkably ineffective at correcting
 misallocation; 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

 unilateral adoption of corrective industrial policies is also ineffective,
 as it leads to immiserizing growth; but 
\begin_inset Formula $(iii)$
\end_inset

 industrial policies coordinated internationally via a deep agreement are
 more transformative than any unilateral policy alternative.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The United States will likely adopt an explicit industrial policy in the
 coming decade.
 Similar developments are well underway in other countries (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "aiginger2020rebirth"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 And with industrial policy back on the scene, we are witnessing a revival
 of old-but-questionable trade policy practices.
 Govenments are often turning to protectionist trade policy measures to
 pursue their industrial policy objectives—as manifested by the United States
 
\emph on
National Trade Council
\emph default
's mission or the Chinese, 
\emph on
Made in China 2025
\emph default
 initiative.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bhagwati1988protectionism"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "irwin2017clashing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a historical account of trade restrictions being used by governments
 to promote their preferred industries.
 A prominent example dates back to 1791, when Alexander Hamilton approached
 Congress with 
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the Report on the Subject of Manufactures
\emph default
,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 encouraging the implementation of protective tariffs and industrial subsidies.
 These policies were intended to help the US economy catch up with Britain.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These developments have sparked new interest in old-but-open questions regarding
 trade and industrial policy.
 For instance: 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{(i)}$
\end_inset

 Is trade policy an effective tool for correcting misallocation in the domestic
 economy? 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{(ii)}$
\end_inset

 If not, should governments undertake 
\emph on
unilateral
\emph default
 domestic policy interventions to correct misallocation? or 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{(iii)}$
\end_inset

 should they coordinate their industrial policies via a deep trade agreement?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To answer these questions, we characterize optimal trade and industrial
 policies in an important class of multi-industry, multi-country quantitative
 trade models where misallocation occurs due to scale economies or profit-genera
ting markups.
 Guided by theory, we estimate the key parameters that govern the welfare
 consequences of trade and industrial policy in open economies.
 We then combine our estimated parameters with optimal policy formulas to
 quantify the ex-ante gains from trade and industrial policy among 43 major
 countries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our estimation reveals that trade policy is remarkably ineffective at correcting
 misallocation, reflecting a deep tension between allocative efficiency
 and terms of trade.
 Unilateral adoption of corrective industrial policies can also backfire
 as it often triggers 
\emph on
immiserizing growth
\emph default
.
 These considerations, we argue, may have spurred a global 
\emph on
race to the bottom
\emph default
, wherein governments either avoid corrective industrial policies or pair
 them with hidden trade barriers.
 A deep agreement can remedy this problem and deliver welfare gains that
 are more transformative than any unilateral policy intervention.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Theoretical-Framework"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 presents our theoretical framework.
 Our baseline model is a generalized multi-industry 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Krugman1980"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model that features a non-parametric utility aggregator across industries
 and a nested CES utility aggregator within industries.
 This specification has an appealing property wherein the degrees of firm-level
 and country-level market love-for-variety can diverge.
 We analyze both the restricted and free entry cases of the model to distinguish
 between the 
\emph on
short-run
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
long-run
\emph default
 consequences of policy.
 With a reinterpretation of parameters, our baseline framework also nests
 
\begin_inset Formula $(a)$
\end_inset

 the multi-industry 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Melitz2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

-Pareto model, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(b)$
\end_inset

 the multi-industry 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Eaton2002"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model with industry-level Marshallian externalities.
 We later extend our baseline model to accommodate non-parametric input-output
 linkages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-Structure-of-Optimal-Policy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 derives sufficient statistics formulas for 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
second-best
\emph default
 trade and industrial policies.
 Unilaterally optimal policies in our framework pursue two objectives: First,
 they seek to improve the home country's
\emph on
 terms-of-trade (ToT)
\emph default
 vis-
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
`a
\end_layout

\end_inset

-vis the rest of the world.
 Second, they seek to restore 
\emph on
allocative efficiency
\emph default
 in the domestic economy by reallocating workers towards high-returns to
 scale or high-profit industries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 optimal policy consists of misallocation-blind import tariffs and export
 subsidies that purely maximize ToT gains.
 Allocative efficiency under the first-best is restored via domestic Pigouvian
 subsidies.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The optimal subsidy rate in each industry equals the inverse of the industry-lev
el scale elasticity or markup.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 While these insights resonate with the targeting principle, our optimal
 policy formulas have other implications worth highlighting.
 First, even though 1st-best trade policies are blind to misallocation (the
 dispersion in scale elasticities), they depend on the overall strength
 of scale economies (the level of scale elasticities).
 Second, the optimal tariff formula is input-output-blind provided that
 export subsidies are assigned optimally.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Specifically, optimal import tariffs are equal to the inverse export supply
 elasticity regardless of input-output relationships.
 Consequently, in the absence of profits and scale economies, optimal tariffs
 become uniform when export subsidies are set optimally—revealing that the
 uniformity result in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "costinot2015comparative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 extends to environments with input-output linkages.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our 
\emph on
second-best
\emph default
 trade policy formulas apply to scenarios where governments are reluctant
 to use industrial subsidies for correcting misallocation—the root of which
 could be political pressures or institutional barriers.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Trade policy has been regularly used—in place of domestic industrial policy—to
 promote critical industries (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bhagwati1988protectionism"
literal "false"

\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "harrison2010trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "irwin2017clashing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Relatedly, see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lane2020new"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a historical account of various industrial policy practices around
 the world.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Second-best trade tax-cum-subsidies are composed of two elements: a neoclassica
l ToT-improving component and a misallocation-correcting component.
 The former aims to restrict relative exports in nationally differentiated
 industries.
 The latter seeks to restrict imports and promote exports in high-returns-to-sca
le industries, mimicking the first-best Pigouvian subsidies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, guided by our optimal policy framework, puts forth two conjectures concerning
 the efficacy of trade and industrial policy: First, if industry-level trade
 and scale elasticities exhibit a strong negative correlation, standalone
 trade policy measures deliver limited welfare gains even when set optimally—ref
lecting their inability to strike a balance between ToT-improving and misallocat
ion-correcting objectives.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In some canonical cases, optimal second-best trade policies are even industry-bl
ind—unable to beneficially correct inter-industry misallocation or manipulate
 the ToT on an industry-by-industry basis.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Second, in these conditions, unilateral scale (or markup) correction via
 industrial policy can cause 
\emph on
immiserizing growth 
\emph default
due to adverse ToT effects.
 Consequently, shallow trade agreements may be insufficient for reaching
 global efficiency.
 Once governments agree to abandon inefficient trade restrictions under
 a shallow agreement, they become tangled in a coordination game involving
 corrective industrial policies.
 The outcome of this game is a 
\emph on
race to the bottom
\emph default
 wherein governments are reluctant to implement corrective industrial policies
 without violating their commitments to free trade.
 A deep agreement can remedy this problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To test these conjectures, Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 estimates the structural parameters necessary for ex-ante policy evaluation.
 Our optimal policy formulas reveal that the sufficient statistics for measuring
 policy outcomes include observables and two sets of parameters: 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 industry-level scale elasticities that govern the extent of misallocation
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

 industry-level trade elasticities that control the scope for ToT manipulation.
 In our generalized 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Krugman1980"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model, scale elasticities reflect the degree of 
\emph on
love-for-variety
\emph default
 whose social benefits are not internalized by firms' entry decisions; and
 trade elasticities represent the degree of national product differentiation.
 We recover both elasticities from firm-level demand parameters, which are
 estimated by fitting a structural demand function to the universe of Colombian
 import transactions covering over 225,000 firms from 251 countries.
 Our identification strategy leverages high-frequency data on import transaction
s and exchange rates to construct a shift-share instrument that measures
 exposure to aggregate exchange rate fluctuations at the firm-product-year
 level.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We develop this identification strategy to overcome challenges that are
 difficult to resolve with standard demand estimation techniques.
 Firm-level demand estimation techniques from the Industrial Organization
 literature leverage information on observed product characteristics, which
 are unavailable at the scale we conduct our estimation.
 Also, unlike traditional 
\emph on
country-level
\emph default
 import demand estimations, we cannot rely on tariff data for identification,
 as tariff rates do not vary sufficiently among firms from the same country.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This estimation strategy is well-suited for our end goal of policy evaluation
 as it separately identifies the scale elasticities from trade elasticities,
 accurately pinpointing their covariance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Quantifying-the-Gains"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 combines our estimated scale and trade elasticity parameters, our optimal
 policy formulas, and macro-level data from the 2014 World Input-Output
 Database to quantify the (maximal) ex-ante gains from policy among 43 major
 economies.
 Our analysis delivers three main findings.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, we find that trade policy is remarkably ineffective at correcting
 misallocation in the domestic economy—even without factoring in the cost
 of retaliation by trading partners.
 Under free entry, second-best export subsidies and import taxes can raise
 the average country's real GDP by only 1.19%, which amounts to less than
 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{4}{10}$
\end_inset

 of the gains attainable under the unilaterally first-best policy.
 Third-best import taxes are even less effective as a standalone policy,
 raising real GDP by a mere 0.63%.
 These findings corroborate the argument that trade policy has difficulty
 striking a balance between ToT and misallocation-correcting objectives.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, the unilateral adoption of corrective industrial policies triggers
 severe immiserizing growth in most countries.
 The average country's real GDP declines by 2.7% if they implement scale-correcti
ng subsidies without reciprocity by trading partners.
 Aversion to these consequences, we argue, may have spurred a global race
 to the bottom in industrial policy implementation.
 To escape immiserizing growth, governments either avoid corrective policies
 or pair them with hidden trade barriers that breach shallow trade cooperation.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The Chinese government, for instance, pairs its domestic subsidies with
 hidden export taxes.
 These hidden barriers are applied via partial value-added tax rebates and
 are designed to restore China's ToT (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "garred2018persistence"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Third, deep agreements can remedy the race to the bottom and deliver welfare
 gains that are more transformative than any unilateral intervention.
 To offer some perspective, corrective industrial policies coordinated via
 a deep agreement can elevate the average country's real GDP by 3.2%.
 These welfare gains rival the already-realized gains from shallow agreements
 for most countries.
 They, moreover, exceed any welfare gains achievable through unilateral
 trade or industrial policy interventions—even not considering that unilateralis
m often backfires in the form of retaliation by trading partners.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Related Literature
\emph default
—Our theory relates to an emerging literature on optimal policy in 
\emph on
distorted
\emph default
 open economies.
 In a concurrent paper, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "BCDR2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 characterize the first-best optimal policy for a small open economy in
 a multi-sector Ricardian model with Marshallian externalities.
 Relatedly, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "haaland2016optimal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 characterize optimal policy for a small open economy in two-by-two Krugman
 and Melitz models.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "demidova2009trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "felbermayr2013optimal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 characterize optimal tariffs in a 
\emph on
single industry
\emph default
 Melitz-Pareto model.
 The single industry assumption ensures that markets are efficient (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "dhingra2019monopolistic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) and import and export taxes are equivalent (the Lerner symmetry).
 So, the unilaterally first-best can be reached with import tariffs alone.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "costinot2016micro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 examine optimal policy in the
\emph on
 single industry
\emph default
 Melitz-Pareto model from a different lens, characterizing optimal 
\emph on
firm-level
\emph default
 taxes.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Beyond optimal policy, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "campolmi2018trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 employ a two-sector Melitz-Pareto model to elucidate the trade-offs facing
 countries that join shallow and deep trade agreements.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Other papers have also used new or quantitative trade models to analyze
 piecemeal policy reforms in distorted economies or optimal policy in 
\emph on
non-distorted
\emph default
 economies—e.g., 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "costinot2014trade,campolmi2014trade,costinot2015comparative,bagwell2015trade,caliendo2015tariff,demidova2017trade,beshkar2017interdependence,beshkar2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Our analysis of 
\emph on
second-best
\emph default
 trade policies speaks to an older literature emphasizing the firm-delocation
 rationale for trade restrictions (e.g., 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "venables1987trade,ossa2011new"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), and supplements 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "bagwell2001domestic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

's 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeyearpar
key "bagwell2001domestic,bagwell2004economics"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 result about the role of trade agreements in distorted economies.
 We also build on 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kucheryavyy2023grounded"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to establish isomorphism between our baseline model and other workhorse
 models in the literature.
 Our 
\emph on
quantitative
\emph default
 examination of trade and industrial policy connects to two strands of literatur
e.
 First, a mature line of research measuring the ex-post consequences of
 tariff cuts (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "costinot2014trade,caliendo2014estimates,ossa2014trade,ossa2016quantitative,spearot2016unpacking"
literal "true"

\end_inset

).
 Second, a growing literature examining the ex-ante consequences of optimal
 policy.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ossa2014trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, most notably, quantifies the consequences of cooperative and non-cooperative
 import tariffs in a multi-industry Krugman model with restricted entry.
 Lastly, our work relates to a vibrant literature examining the impacts
 of exogenous trade shocks in distorted economies (e.g., 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "de2015understanding"
literal "false"

\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "edmond2015competition"
literal "false"

\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "baqaee2019networks"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We contribute to the calculus of optimal policy in open economies by developing
 a new dual technique for optimal policy derivation in general equilibrium
 quantitative trade models with many countries, increasing returns-to-scale
 production technologies, and input-output linkages.
 Our approach has applications beyond those considered in this paper.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lashkaripour2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, for instance, adopts a special case of this technique to characterize
 Nash tariffs in a monopolistic competition model with restricted entry.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lashkaripour2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 examines the cost of non-cooperative import restrictions when governments
 simultaneously apply their 3rd-best optimal import tariffs.
 To expedite the computational process, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lashkaripour2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 uses analytic formulas for Nash tariffs, which correspond to a special
 of our Theorem 3.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "farrokhi2021can"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 extend this technique to analyze optimal carbon pricing under international
 climate externalities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lastly, our paper supplements the broader quantitative trade literature
 by developing an estimation technique that separately identifies the scale
 elasticity from the trade elasticity in certain environments.
 Our indirect approach to scale elasticity estimation complements the direct
 method concurrently proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "BCDR2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The main limitation of our indirect approach is that it cannot detect scale
 externalities unrelated to love-for-variety, as it does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 directly leverage scale-related moments.
 Despite this limitation, our approach has two useful properties for policy
 evaluation.
 First, it separately identifies the trade elasticity from the scale elasticity,
 provided that scale economies arise from love-for-variety, which is valuable
 since the covariance between these elasticities regulates policy outcomes.
 Second, our approach is robust to the presence of quasi-fixed production
 inputs, enabling us to isolate scale effects that impair allocative efficiency
 from fixed-input-driven diseconomies of scale.
