\begin_layout Standard
We consider a world economy consisting of multiple countries and industries.
 Countries are indexed by of 
\begin_inset Formula $i,j,n\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

.
 Industries are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $g,k\in\mathbb{K}$
\end_inset

.
 Industries can differ in fundamentals such as the degree of scale economies
 or trade elasticity.
 Each country 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 is populated by 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{i}$
\end_inset

 individuals who supply one unit of labor inelasticity.
 Labor is the sole primary factor of production in each economy.
 Workers cannot relocate between countries but are perfectly mobile across
 industries within a country, and are paid a country-wide wage, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each good in our model is indexed by a triplet, which signifies its location
 of production (origin), it location of final consumption (destination),
 and the industry under which the good is classified.
 To give an example: Good 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 denotes a good corresponding to 
\emph on
origin country 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–destination country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
\begin_inset VSpace bigskip
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\emph on
Cross-Industry Demand
\emph default
.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

The representative consumer in country 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 faces a vector of industry-level consumer price indexes 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{\ensuremath{\tilde{\textbf{P}}}}_{i}=\{\tilde{P}_{i,k}\}$
\end_inset

, where index 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{i,k}\equiv\tilde{P}_{i,k}(\tilde{P}_{1i,k},...,\tilde{P}_{Ni,k})$
\end_inset

 aggregates over industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 goods sourced from various origins.
 The consumer chooses their demand for industry-level bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{Q}_{i}\equiv\{Q_{i,k}\}$
\end_inset

 to maximize a non-parametric utility function subject to a budget constraint.
 This choice yields an indirect utility, which is a function of the consumer's
 income, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{i}$
\end_inset

, and the vector of industry-level 
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

consumer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\emph default
 price indexes in market 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
V_{i}\left(Y_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}\right) & =\max_{\textbf{Q}_{i}}\ U_{i}(\textbf{Q}_{i})\ \ \ \ \ \ \ s.t.\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\tilde{P}_{i,k}Q_{i,k}=Y_{i}.\label{eq: Optimal Consumption}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Throughout this paper, the 
\emph on
tilde
\emph default
 notation on price is used to distinguish between 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

consumer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

producer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 prices.
 The former includes taxes, whereas the latter does not.
 Problem 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Optimal Consumption"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 yields an 
\emph on
industry-level
\emph default
 Marshallian demand function, which we denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i,k}=\mathcal{D}_{i,k}\left(Y_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 This function tracks how (given prices and total income) consumers allocate
 their expenditure across industries.
 A special case of our general cross-industry demand function is the Cobb-Dougla
s case, wherein 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i}(\textbf{Q}_{i})=\prod_{k\in\mathbb{K}}Q_{i,k}^{e_{i,k}}$
\end_inset

 implying that 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i,k}=e_{i,k}Y_{i}/\tilde{P}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset VSpace bigskip
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\emph on
Within-Industry Demand
\emph default
.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Each industry-level bundle aggregates over various origin-specific composite
 varieties: 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i,k}\equiv Q_{i,k}(Q_{1i,k},...,Q_{Ni,k})$
\end_inset

.
 Each origin-specific composite variety, itself, aggregates over multiple
 firm-level varieties: 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{ji,k}\equiv Q_{ji,k}(\textbf{q}_{ji,k})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{q}_{ji,k}=\left\{ q_{ji,k}\left(\omega\right)\right\} _{\omega\in\Omega_{j,k}}$
\end_inset

 is a vector with each element 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{ji,k}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 denoting the quantity consumed of firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

's output.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{j,k}$
\end_inset

 denotes the set of all firms operating in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 In our baseline model, firms do 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 incur a fixed exporting cost, so each firm in 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{j,k}$
\end_inset

 serves market 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 We relax this assumption in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Extensions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We assume that the within-industry utility aggregator has a nested-CES
 structure, which enables us to abstract from variable markups and direct
 our attention to the scale-driven and profit-shifting effects of policy.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
medskip
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
textbf{Assumption (A1).}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\ $
\end_inset

 The within-industry utility aggregator is nested-CES.
 In particular,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q_{i,k}=\left(\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}Q_{ji,k}^{\frac{\sigma_{k}-1}{\sigma_{k}}}\right)^{\frac{\sigma_{k}}{\sigma_{k}-1}},\qquad\text{where}\qquad Q_{ji,k}=\left(\int_{\omega\in\Omega_{j,k}}\varphi_{ji,k}(\omega)^{\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}}}q_{ji,k}\left(\omega\right)^{\frac{\gamma_{k-}1}{\gamma_{k}}}d\omega\right)^{\frac{\gamma_{k}}{\gamma_{k}-1}},
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}\geq\sigma_{k}>1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{ji,k}(\omega)>0$
\end_inset

 corresponding to a constant variety-specific taste shifter.
 
\emph default

\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
medskip
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on (A1), the demand for the composite 
\emph on
national-level
\emph default
 variety 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 (origin country 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–destination country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

) is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Q_{ji,k}=\left(\tilde{P}_{ji,k}/\tilde{P}_{i,k}\right)^{-\sigma_{k}}Q_{i,k},\label{eq: Country-Level Demand Function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 respectively denote the origin-specific and industry-level CES price indexes.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{ji,k}=\left(\sum_{\omega\in\Omega_{ji,k}}\varphi_{ji,k}(\omega)\tilde{p}_{ji,k}\left(\omega\right)^{1-\gamma_{k}}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\gamma_{k}}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{i,k}=\left(\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}\tilde{P}_{ji,k}^{1-\sigma_{k}}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma_{k}}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 denotes industry-level demand, which is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{i,k}=\mathcal{D}_{i,k}\left(Y_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The demand facing individual firms from country 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is, accordingly, given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{ji,k}\left(\omega\right)=\varphi_{ji,k}(\omega)\left(\frac{\tilde{p}_{ji,k}\left(\omega\right)}{\tilde{P}_{ji,k}}\right)^{-\gamma_{k}}\left(\frac{\tilde{P}_{ji,k}}{\tilde{P}_{i,k}}\right)^{-\sigma_{k}}\mathcal{D}_{i,k}\left(Y_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}\right).\label{eq: Firm-Level Demand Function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Importantly, the above parameterization of demand allows for the 
\emph on
firm-level
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
national-level
\emph default
 degrees of market power to diverge.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}$
\end_inset

 governs the degree of firm-level market power and love-for-variety, while
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 governs the degree of national-level market power in industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 
\emph on

\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\emph default

\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\emph on
Elasticity of Demand Facing National-Level Varieties.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Following Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Country-Level Demand Function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the demand for aggregate variety 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 is a function of total income in market 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{i}$
\end_inset

, and the entire vector of 
\emph on
origin
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

industry
\emph default
-specific consumer price indexes in that market: Namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{ji,k}=\mathcal{D}_{ji,k}\left(Y_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 To keep track of changes in demand, we define the elasticity of demand
 for national-level variety 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 w.r.t.
 to the price of variety 
\begin_inset Formula $ni,g$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varepsilon_{ji,k}^{(ni,g)}\equiv\frac{\partial\ln\mathcal{\mathcal{D}}_{ji,k}(Y_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i})}{\partial\ln\tilde{P}_{ni,g}}\sim\textrm{price elasticity of demand}
\]

\end_inset

Under Cobb-Douglas preferences (i.e., zero cross-substitutability between
 industries), the national-level demand elasticities are fully determined
 by the upper-tier CES parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and national-level expenditure shares.
 Specifically, 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{ji,k}^{\jmath i,g}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $g\neq k$
\end_inset

, while
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\varepsilon_{ji,k}\sim\varepsilon_{ji,k}^{(ji,k)}=-1-(\sigma_{k}-1)(1-\lambda_{ji,k});\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \varepsilon_{ji,k}^{(\jmath i,k)}=(\sigma_{k}-1)\lambda_{\jmath i,k}\ \ \ \ (\jmath\neq j),
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{ji,k}\equiv\tilde{P}_{ji,k}Q_{ji,k}/\sum_{\jmath}\tilde{P}_{\jmath i,k}Q_{\jmath i,k}$
\end_inset

 denotes the (within-industry) share of expenditure on 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

.
 In the presence of cross-substitutability between industries, the demand
 elasticity will feature an additional term that accounts for cross-industry
 demand effects.
\end_layout

