\begin_layout Subsection
Production and Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each economy 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 is populated with a mass 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{i,k}=\left|\Omega_{i,k}\right|$
\end_inset

 of single-product firms in industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\mathbb{K}$
\end_inset

 that compete under monopolistic competition.
 Labor is the only factor of production.
 Firm entry into industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is either free or restricted.
 Under restricted entry, 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{i,k}=\overline{M}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 is invariant to policy.
 Under free entry, a pool of ex-ante identical firms can pay an entry cost
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}f_{k}^{e}$
\end_inset

 to serve industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 from origin 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 After paying the entry cost, each firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega\in\Omega_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 draws a productivity 
\begin_inset Formula $z(\omega)\geq1$
\end_inset

 from distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{i,k}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

, and faces a marginal cost 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{ij,k}w_{i}/z\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 for producing and delivering goods to destination 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{ij,k}$
\end_inset

 denotes a flat iceberg transport cost.
 Collecting these assumptions, the 
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

producer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\emph default
 price index of composite good 
\begin_inset Formula $ij,k$
\end_inset

 (which aggregates over firm-level varieties associated with 
\emph on
origin 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

–destination 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\emph default
) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{ij,k}=\frac{\gamma_{k}}{\gamma_{k}-1}\tau_{ij,k}\bar{a}_{i,k}w_{i}M_{i,k}^{-\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}},\label{eq: Producer Price A2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{a}_{i,k}\equiv\left[\int_{1}^{\infty}z{}^{\gamma_{k}-1}dG_{i,k}(z)\right]^{\frac{1}{1-\gamma_{k}}}$
\end_inset

 denotes the average unit labor cost in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{a}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 is constant in our baseline model.
 This is no longer true in the 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Melitz2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 extension of oue model explored in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Extensions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, in which firms incur a fixed cost to serve individual markets.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kucheryavyy2023grounded"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, we refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}=-\frac{\partial\ln P_{ij,k}}{\partial\ln M_{i,k}}$
\end_inset

 as the industry-level 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
textbf{
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph on
scale elasticity
\emph default

\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{k}\equiv\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}\sim\textrm{scale elasticity}\sim\textrm{markup}
\]

\end_inset

Considering Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Producer Price A2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 represents both 
\begin_inset Formula $(a)$
\end_inset

 the constant firm-level markup in industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 (
\emph on
i.e.
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\mu_{k}=\frac{\gamma_{k}}{\gamma_{k}-1}$
\end_inset

), and 
\begin_inset Formula $(b)$
\end_inset

 the elasticity by which (variety-adjusted) TFP increases with industry-level
 employment 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 (noting that 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{i,k}\propto M_{i,k}$
\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
With free entry and constant markups, it follows immediately that 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{i,k}=\bar{c}_{i,k}M_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{c}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 is a constant.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The equivalence between markup and scale elasticity is not a universal
 property but a specific feature of our baseline Krugman model.
 We take advantage of this equivalence to simplify notation, but it is not
 essential for the theoretical results that follow.
 As shown in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Extensions"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, our analytical formulas for optimal policy extend to alternative models
 where the scale elasticity and markup levels diverge.
\emph on

\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

Expressing Producer Prices in terms of Profit-Adjusted Wages.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
Our optimal policy framework reveals a tight connection between the restricted
 and free entry cases—even though misallocation stems from markup distortions
 in the former and scale distortions in the latter.
 To illustrate this connection and integrate optimal policy results for
 the two cases, we specify producer prices as a function of profit-adjusted
 wage rates.
 The idea is that net profits (if any) are rebated back to workers.
 The profit-adjusted wage rate in country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\grave{w}{}_{i}\equiv(1+\overline{\mu}_{i})w_{i}\sim\textrm{profit-adjusted wage},
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes economy 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's average profit margin across all industries.
 Namely,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\overline{\mu}_{i}=\begin{cases}
0 & \textrm{if entry is free}\\
\frac{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}\frac{\mu_{k}}{1+\mu_{k}}P_{ij,k}Q_{ij,k}}{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}\frac{1}{1+\mu_{k}}P_{ij,k}Q_{ij,k}} & \textrm{if entry is restricted}
\end{cases}.\label{eq: profist margin}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Under free entry, profits are drawn to zero, resulting in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{i}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Under restricted entry, the average profit margin is positive and depends
 on the industrial composition of country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's output—with a higher 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{i}$
\end_inset

 reflecting more sales in high-markup (high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

) industries.
 Appealing to our definitions for 
\begin_inset Formula $\grave{w}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

, we can reformulate Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Producer Price A2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 to express producer prices as a function of 
\emph on
profit-adjusted
\emph default
 wages:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{ij,k}=\begin{cases}
\varrho{}_{ij,k}\ \left[\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}\tau_{ij,k}Q_{ij,k}\right]^{-\frac{\mu_{k}}{1+\mu_{k}}}\grave{w}{}_{i} & \textrm{if entry is free}\\
\varrho'_{ij,k}\ \frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}\ \grave{w}{}_{i} & \textrm{if entry is restricted}
\end{cases}.\label{eq: Producer Price}
\end{equation}

