\begin_layout Subsection
The Instruments of Policy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government in country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has is afforded a complete set of revenue-raising trade and domestic policy
 instruments; namely,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
import tax,
\emph default
 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $t_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

, applied to all goods imported from origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq i$
\end_inset

 in industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
export subsidy
\series default
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij,k}$
\end_inset

, applied to all goods sold to market 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq i$
\end_inset

 in industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
\emph on
industrial subsidy
\series default
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i,k}$
\end_inset

, applied to industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

's output irrespective of where it is sold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our specification of policy is quite flexible as it accommodates import
 subsidies or export taxes (
\begin_inset Formula $-1\leq t<0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-1\leq x<0$
\end_inset

) as well as production taxes (
\begin_inset Formula $-1\leq s<0$
\end_inset

).
 We disregard 
\emph on
consumption taxes
\emph default
 as they are redundant given the availability of the other tax instruments
 (see online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Replicating-Consumption-Taxes"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 There is a simple intuition behind this redundancy: Country 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 has access to 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)+2$
\end_inset

 different tax instruments in each industry (where 
\begin_inset Formula $N\equiv\left|\mathbb{C}\right|$
\end_inset

 denotes the number of countries).
 These 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)+2$
\end_inset

 tax instruments can directly manipulate 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)+1$
\end_inset

 consumer price indexes: 
\begin_inset Formula $N-1$
\end_inset

 export prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $N-1$
\end_inset

 import prices, and one price associated with the domestically-produced
 and consumed variety (namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{ii,k}$
\end_inset

).
 So, by construction, one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)+2$
\end_inset

 tax instruments in each industry is redundant.
 Here, we treat the industry-level consumption tax as a redundant instrument.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
With more than two countries (
\begin_inset Formula $N>2)$
\end_inset

, Country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has access to 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)+2$
\end_inset

 instruments per industry.
 These instruments can manipulate 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)+1$
\end_inset

 price variables, which implies the same redundancy.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above tax instruments create a wedge between consumer price indexes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\tilde{P}_{ji,k}\}$
\end_inset

 and producer price indexes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\{P_{ji,k}\}$
\end_inset

, as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\tilde{P}_{ji,k}=\frac{1+t_{ji,k}}{(1+x_{ji,k})(1+s_{j,k})}P_{ji.k},\ \ \ \ \forall j,i\in\mathbb{C},\ k\in\mathbb{K}.\label{eq: Consumer Price}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

These tax instruments also generate/exhaust revenue for the tax-imposing
 country.
 The combination of all taxes imposed by country 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 produce a tax revenue equal to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}_{i}=\  & \overbrace{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\left(\left(\frac{1}{1+s_{i,k}}-1\right)P_{ii,k}Q_{ii,k}\right)}^{\text{industrial subsidies}}\ \nonumber \\
+ & \underbrace{\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\sum_{j\neq i}\left(\frac{t_{ji,k}}{(1+x_{ji,k})(1+s_{j,k})}P_{ji,k}Q_{ji,k}+\left[\frac{1}{(1+x_{ij,k})(1+s_{i,k})}-1\right]P_{ij,k}Q_{ij,k}\right)}_{\text{import taxes + export subsidies}}.\label{eq: tax revenue}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Tax revenues are rebated to the consumers in a lump-sum fashion.
 After we account for tax revenues, total income in country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 equals the sum of profit-adjusted wage payments, 
\begin_inset Formula $\grave{w}_{i}L_{i}=(1+\overline{\mu}_{i})w_{i}L_{i}$
\end_inset

, and tax revenues.
 Namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{i}=\grave{w}_{i}L_{i}+\mathcal{R}_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be positive or negative depending on whether country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's policy consists of net taxes or subsidies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
