\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:The-Structure-of-Optimal-Policy"

\end_inset

Sufficient Statistics Formulas for Optimal Policy 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This section derives sufficient statistics formulas for optimal trade and
 industrial policies.
 These formulas are later employed to quantify the ex-ante gains from policy
 among many countries.
 Before proceeding to the derivation, let us review the two rationales for
 policy intervention in our setup.
 A non-cooperative, welfare-maximizing government seeks to 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 restrict trade and reap unexploited 
\emph on
terms-of-trade (ToT)
\emph default
 gains 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

vis-
\backslash
`a-vis
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the rest of the world, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

 correct misallocation in the domestic economy.
 Misallocation, notice, stems from the cross-industry heterogeneity in markups
 or scale elasticities, leading to inefficiently low output in high-profit
 or high-returns-to-scale (high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

) industries.
 A crucial difference between these policy objectives is that ToT manipulation
 is inefficient from a global standpoint, as it distorts international prices
 to transfer surplus from the rest of the world to the tax-imposing country.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Efficient Policy from a Global Standpoint
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a useful benchmark, we first characterize the efficient policy from a
 global standpoint.
 Efficient policies, by definition, are the solution to a central planner's
 problem that maximizes global welfare via taxes and lump-sum international
 transfers.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote the Pareto weight assigned to country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the planner's objective function.
 The globally efficient policy solves the following planning problem 
\emph on
subject to
\emph default
 the availability of lump-sum transfers:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\left(\textbf{t},\textbf{x},\textbf{s};\textbf{w}\right)\in\mathbb{F}}\ \ \ \sum_{i\in\mathbb{C}}\delta_{i}\log W_{i}\left(\textbf{t},\textbf{x},\textbf{s};\textbf{w}\right).
\]

\end_inset

Keep in mind that the above problem affords the planner enough instruments
 to obtain their first-best.
 Good-specific taxes allow the planner to restore allocative efficiency,
 while lump-sum transfers allow her to redistribute inter-nationally based
 on the Pareto weights, 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 This point is expanded on in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Globally-Efficient-Policy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, were it is shown that the efficient tax policy involves 
\emph on
zero
\emph default
 trade taxes and Pigouvian subsidies that restore marginal-cost-pricing
 globally:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To be specific, the implementation of the efficient allocation involves
 the above taxes plus lump-sum international transfers based on Pareto weights.
 The logic is that the planner maximizes global output by restoring marginal-cos
t pricing and redistributes the corresponding income gains between countries
 via efficient transfers.
 Absent transfers, implementing 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \textbf{t}^{\star},\textbf{x}^{\star},\textbf{s}^{\star}\right\} $
\end_inset

 would deliver a Kaldor-Hicks improvement (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kaldor1939welfare,hicks1939foundations"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), but not necessarily a Pareto improvement relative to Laissez-faire—see
 online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Globally-Efficient-Policy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for more details.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
t_{ji,k}^{\star}=x_{ji,k}^{\star}=0\ \ \ \forall ji,k;\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1+s_{i,k}^{\star} & =1+\mu_{k}\ \ \ \forall i,k\label{eq: deep cooperation}
\end{align}

\end_inset

The above characterization applies to both the free and restricted entry
 cases—with the understanding that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 assumes different interpretations in each case.
 Appealing to this result, online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: App Theorem 1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 establishes a basic point about international cooperation: Welfare-maximizing
 governments will settle on the efficient policy only if they are unable
 to influence consumer/producer prices in the rest of the world.
 Otherwise, they will defect to take advantage of terms-of-trade (ToT) gains.
 This result indicates that the pursuit of ToT gains is the sole reason
 welfare-maximizing governments deviate from efficient policy choices—at
 least when they are afforded sufficient policy instruments.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This need not be true if government are prohibited from using domestic taxes
 and afforded only import tax instruments.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "venables1987trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ossa2011new"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, welfare-maximizing governments will erect tariffs in that case, even if
 they perceive world prices as invariant to their policy choice.
 By doing so, they improve allocative efficiency in the domestic economy
 but impose a negative firm-delocation externality on the rest of the world.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This result echos the argument in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "bagwell2001domestic,bagwell2004economics"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, generalizing it to settings with many countries and differentiated industries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the next section, we characterize the unilaterally optimal policy of
 non-cooperative governments.
 This exercise elucidates two issues.
 First, it determines how governments deviate from the cooperative policy
 choice when ToT considerations are taken into account.
 Second, it clarifies how governments approach industrial policy when they
 view 1st-best Pigouvian subsidies as politically infeasible.
 Once we settle these two issues, we argue that the implementation of globally
 efficient policies requires both a 
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

shallow agreement
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\emph default
 to discipline trade policy choices and a 
\emph on

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

deep agreement
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\emph default
 to coordinate industrial policy implementation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Unilaterally Optimal Policy Choices
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
First-Best: Unilaterally Optimal Trade and Domestic Policies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now characterize a non-cooperative country’s 
\emph on
unilaterally
\emph default
 optimal policy.
 We consider cases where a non-cooperative country 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 selects taxes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{t}_{i}\equiv\{t_{ji,k}\}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{x}_{i}\equiv\{x_{ij,k}\}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{s}_{i}\equiv\{s_{i,k}\}$
\end_inset

, taking policy choices elsewhere as given.
 Countries in the rest of the world are passive in their use of taxes but
 actively maintain internal cooperation.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
One aspect of internal cooperation requires further clarification: Country
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

’s policy could, in principle, disrupt the balance of market access concessions
 in the rest of the world, leading to a deterioration of one cooperative
 country’s ToT relative to another.
 Cooperation 
\emph on
within
\emph default
 the rest of the world entails that these extraterritorial ToT effects be
 neutralized via buffers that preserve 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{n}/w_{j}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n,j\neq i$
\end_inset

—see online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Policy Buffers"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for further details.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We begin with the unilaterally 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 case where the government in 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is afforded all possible tax instruments.
 The first-best unilaterally optimal policy solves the following problem:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Given that the rest of the world is passive in their use of taxes (i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{t}_{-i}=\textbf{x}_{-i}=\textbf{s}_{-i}=\textbf{0}$
\end_inset

), we condense the notation by specifying equilibrium variables as a function
 of only 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i},\textbf{w}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)}\ \ \ W_{i}\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)\ \ \ \ \ \ s.t.\ \left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)\in\mathbb{F}\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ (\textrm{P1}).
\]

\end_inset

We analytically solve Problem (P1) under both the restricted and free entry
 cases.
 We perceive the restricted entry case to be a more appropriate benchmark
 if governments are concerned with 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
textbf{
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph on
short-run
\emph default

\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 gains from policy.
 The free entry case, on the other hand, is more relevant if governments
 are concerned with 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
textbf{
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph on
long-run
\emph default

\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 gains.
 These two cases exhibit an important difference: Producer prices respond
 differently to contractions in export supply under restricted and free
 entry—as we elaborate next.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\emph on
Conditional Export Supply Elasticity.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

The terms-of-trade gains from policy, in our framework, channel through
 changes in the price of imported and exported goods.
 The government in 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 cannot directly dictate the 
\emph on
producer
\emph default
 price of say good, 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

, that is imported from origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j\neq i$
\end_inset

.
 Instead, it can deflate its producer price (
\begin_inset Formula $P_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

) 
\emph on
indirectly
\emph default
 by contracting or expanding its export supply (
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

).
 The contraction in 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

 also affects the producer price of goods supplied by other locations through
 general equilibrium linkages.
 Our theory indicates that, for optimal policy analysis, the 
\emph on
conditional inverse export supply elasticity 
\emph default
is sufficient to track these effects.
 To present this elasticity, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\mathbb{P}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 contain the consumer price of all goods either produced by or consumed
 in country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 These are prices that country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's government can fully control via taxes.
 We define the 
\emph on
conditional
\emph default
 inverse export supply elasticity of good 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\omega_{ji,k}\equiv\frac{1}{r_{ji,k}\rho_{j,k}}\sum_{g\in\mathbb{K}}\left[\frac{\grave{w}_{i}L_{i}}{\grave{w}_{j}L_{j}}\rho_{i,g}\left(\frac{\partial\ln P_{ii,g}}{\partial\ln Q_{ji,k}}\right)_{\textbf{w},\textbf{Y},\tilde{\mathbb{P}}_{i}}+\sum_{n\neq i}\frac{\grave{w}_{n}L_{n}}{\grave{w}_{j}L_{j}}r_{ni,g}\rho_{n,g}\left(\frac{\partial\ln P_{ni,g}}{\partial\ln Q_{ji,k}}\right)_{\textbf{w},\textbf{Y},\tilde{\mathbb{P}}_{i}}\right],
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{ni,g}\equiv\frac{P_{ni,g}Q_{ni,g}}{\sum_{\iota}P_{n\iota,g}Q_{n\iota,g}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{n,g}\equiv\frac{\sum_{\iota}P_{n\iota,g}Q_{\iota,g}}{\sum_{\iota,s}P_{n\iota,s}Q_{\iota,s}}$
\end_inset

 respectively denote the 
\emph on
good-specific
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
industry-wide
\emph default
 sales shares associated with origin 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 Notice, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

 is a conditional elasticity that describes how the 
\emph on
producer
\emph default
 prices linked to economy 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 respond to a change in 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

, holding 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\mathbb{P}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the entire vector of wage and income levels constant.
 This elasticity encapsulates different economic forces under free and restricte
d entry, as we detail next.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under restricted entry, producer prices from origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 are fully determined by the (profit-adjusted) wage rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\grave{w}_{j}$
\end_inset

, and the aggregate profit margin, 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{j}$
\end_inset

 (see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Producer Price"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Policy, thus, has two distinct effects on producer prices under restricted
 entry: One effect that channels through wages, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{w}$
\end_inset

; and another that channels through aggregate profit margins.
 To explain the latter, hold 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{w}$
\end_inset

 constant: contracting the export supply of good 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 with taxes will alter all producer prices associated with origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 through a change in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

's aggregate profit margin, 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 The change in 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{j}$
\end_inset

 derives from the fact that industries have differential markup margins,
 and that taxing good 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 alters the industrial composition of output in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under free entry, producer prices from origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j\in\mathbb{C}$
\end_inset

 are determined by the wage rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\grave{w}_{j}$
\end_inset

, and the 
\emph on
origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–industry
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-specific scale of production.
 So, aside from wage-related effects, policy has a second effect on producer
 prices that channels through industry-level scale economies.
 To elaborate, consider an import tax on good 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 (origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–destination 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

).
 Such a tax contracts the supply of 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 and the scale of production in 
\emph on
origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
 Given Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Producer Price"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, this contraction in scale increases the entire vector of producer price
 indexes associated with 
\emph on
origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\emph default
—all through additional firm entry.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In both cases, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

 describes how expanding or contracting good 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

's export supply impacts country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's terms-of-trade via either profit-shifting or industry-level scale economies.
 Importantly, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

 can be characterized (to a first-order approximation) as a simple function
 of sales shares, scale elasticities, and Marshallian demand elasticities
 (see online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: App Theorem 1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The above approximation derives from 
