\emph on
Three-Step Dual Approach to Characterizing Optimal Policy.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
Our characterization of optimal policy employs the dual approach and is
 presented in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: App Theorem 1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 Below, we provide a verbal summary of our approach, which involves three
 main steps.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
First
\emph default
, we simplify Problem (P1) by reformulating it into a problem where country
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's government chooses the vector of prices 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\mathbb{P}}_{i}=\left\{ \tilde{\textbf{P}}_{ii},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{ji},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{ij}\right\} $
\end_inset

 associated with its own economy.
 Country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's optimal tax/subsidy schedule 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{T}_{i}^{*}\equiv\left(\textbf{t}_{i}^{*},\textbf{x}_{i}^{*},\textbf{s}_{i}^{*}\right)$
\end_inset

 is then recovered as the wedge between the optimal price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{\tilde{P}}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

and producer prices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Second
\emph default
, we derive the first-order conditions (F.O.C.) associated with country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

’s reformulated optimal policy problem.
 We use two technical tricks to overcome the complications related to general
 equilibrium analysis: First, we use the envelope conditions associated
 with optimal demand choices to net out redundant behavioral responses.
 Second, we identify additional welfare neutrality conditions specific to
 Problem (P1).
 Most importantly, we observe that terms in the F.O.C.s that account for general
 equilibrium wage and income effects are redundant in the neighborhood of
 the optimum.
 That is, we could specify the F.O.C.s associated with (P1) 
\emph on
as if
\emph default
 wages were constant and Marshallian demand functions were income-inelastic.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
