\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)}\ \ \ W_{i}\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)\ \ \ \ \ s.t.\ \begin{cases}
\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)\in\mathbb{F}\\
\textbf{s}_{i}=\textbf{0}
\end{cases}\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ (\textrm{P2}).
\]

\end_inset

Using the dual approach discussed earlier, we analytically solve Problem
 (P2) and derive sufficient statistics formulas for second-best optimal
 trade taxes.
 The following theorem presents these formulas, with a formal proof provided
 in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: App Theorem 2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Suppose the government is unable (or unwilling) to apply domestic industrial
 subsidies.
 In that case, the second-best optimal import tariffs and export subsidies
 are unique up to a uniform tax shifter 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{t}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and are implicitly given by:
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
[\text{import tariff}]\ \ \ \ \ \ \textbf{1}+\textbf{t}_{ji}^{**} & =\left(1+\bar{t}_{i}\right)(\textbf{1}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{ji})\oslash\left(\textbf{1}+\textbf{E}_{-ii}^{-1}\ \textbf{E}_{-ii}^{(ii)}\ \left[1-\frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}\right]_{k}\right)\\{}
[\text{export subsidy}]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \textbf{1}+\textbf{x}_{ij}^{**} & =-(1+\bar{t}_{i})\left(\textbf{E}_{ij}^{-1}\textbf{E}_{ij}^{(-ij)}(\textbf{1}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{-ii})\right)\odot\left[\frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}\right]_{k},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\emph default
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{ji}=\left[\omega_{ji,k}\right]_{k}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of inverse export supply elasticities (Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: XSE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

); 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the output-weighted average markup in economy 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 (Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: profist margin"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

); and
\emph on
 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{-ii}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{-ii}^{(ii)}$
\end_inset

,
\emph default
 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{ij}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{ij}^{(-ij)}$
\end_inset


\emph default
 are matrixes of Marshallian demand elasticities as defined under Definition
 (D1).
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
lineskip=-2pt
\end_layout

\end_inset

Letting 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 denote the number of countries and industries: 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{-ii}\sim\textbf{E}_{-ii}^{(-ii)}=\left[\textbf{E}_{ni}^{(\jmath i)}\right]_{n\neq i,\jmath\neq i}$
\end_inset

 is a square 
\begin_inset Formula $(N-1)K\times(N-1)K$
\end_inset

 matrix, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{ni}^{(\jmath i)}\equiv\left[\varepsilon_{ni,k}^{(\jmath i,g)}\right]_{k,g}$
\end_inset

 as defined under Definition (D1).
 Likewise, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{ij}^{(-ij)}=\left[\textbf{E}_{ij}^{(nj)}\right]_{n\neq i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{-ii}^{(ii)}=\left[\textbf{E}_{ni}^{(ii)}\right]_{n\neq i}$
\end_inset

 
\emph default
are respectively 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times(N-1)K$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(N-1)K\times K$
\end_inset

 matrixes.
 In all the equations, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{1}\equiv\textbf{1}_{(N-1)K\times1}$
\end_inset

 is a columns vector of ones.
 Meanwhile, 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{-ii}=\left[\omega_{ni,k}\right]_{n\neq i,k}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $(N-1)K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector; and the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\odot$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\oslash$
\end_inset

 denote element-wise multiplication and division.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Theorem 2 asserts that, when governments cannot use industrial subsidies,
 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 the optimal export subsidy is adjusted to promote exports in high-returns-to-sc
ale (high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

) industries, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

 the optimal import tax is adjusted to restrict import competition in high-retur
ns-to-scale (high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

) industries.
 Intuitively, the government's objective when solving (P2) is to mimic Pigouvian
 industrial subsidies with trade taxes/subsidies.
 To reach this objective, import taxes and export subsidies should increase
 in high-returns-to-scale industries relative to the 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 benchmark.
 While these adjustments elevate domestic production in high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 industries, they are insufficient for obtaining the unilaterally first-best
 allocation.
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As noted earlier, the government needs at least 
\begin_inset Formula $2(N-1)K+1$
\end_inset

 tax instruments (per industry) to achieve the first-best outcome.
 In 
\emph on
homogeneous
\emph default
 good models, though, trade taxes can fully mimic industrial subsidies,
 because fewer instruments suffice to achieve the first-best.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace bigskip
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\emph on
Special Case with Cobb-Douglas Preferences across Industries.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
We can invoke the Cobb-Douglas assumption to further elucidate the second-best
 tax formulas under Theorem 2.
 Under this assumption, there are zero cross-demand effects between industries
 and the optimal policy formulas specified by Theorem 2 can be simplified
 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
[\text{import tariff}]\ \ \ \ \ \ 1+t_{ji,k}^{**} & =\frac{1+\left(\sigma_{k}-1\right)\lambda_{ii,k}}{1+\frac{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}{1+\mu_{k}}\left(\sigma_{k}-1\right)\lambda_{ii,k}}\ \left(1+t_{ji,k}^{*}\right)\\{}
[\text{export subsidy}]\ \ \ \ \ \ 1+x_{ij,k}^{**} & =\frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}\ \left(1+x_{ij,k}^{*}\right),
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $1+t_{ji,k}^{*}=(1+\omega_{ji,k})(1+\bar{t}_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $1+x_{ji,k}^{*}=\frac{(\sigma_{k}-1)\sum_{n\neq i}\left[(1+\omega_{ni,k})\lambda_{nj,k}\right]}{1+(\sigma_{k}-1)(1-\lambda_{ij,k})}(1+\bar{t}_{i})$
\end_inset

 denote the first-best optimal rate (Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: 1st best CD"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The small open economy case of the above formulas can be stated as follows:
