}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\emph on
,
\emph default
 the formulas further reduce to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1+t_{ji,k}^{**}=\frac{1+(\sigma_{k}-1)\lambda_{ii,k}}{1+\frac{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}{1+\mu_{k}}(\sigma_{k}-1)\lambda_{ii,k}}(1+\bar{t}_{i});\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1+x_{ij,k}^{**}=\frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}\left(\frac{\sigma_{k}-1}{\sigma_{k}}\right)(1+\bar{t}_{i}).
\]

\end_inset

In summary, the above formulas indicate that second-best 
\emph on
import
\emph default
 taxes are higher in 
\begin_inset Formula $(1)$
\end_inset

 industries with a greater-than-average markup, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(2)$
\end_inset

 industries in which country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 has a comparative advantage (i.e., high-
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma_{k}-1)\lambda_{ii,k}$
\end_inset

 industries).
 These two properties allow second-best import taxes to mimic Pigouvian
 subsidies to the best extent possible.
 Likewise, second-best 
\emph on
export
\emph default
 subsidies feature a 
\emph on
misallocation-correcting
\emph default
 component that favors industries with a higher-than-average scale elasticity
 or markup.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Importantly, if the markup or scale elasticity is uniform across industries
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=\mu=\overline{\mu}_{i}$
\end_inset

), the above formulas yield the 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 or purely ToT-improving tax rate—i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{ji,k}^{**}=t_{ji,k}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij,k}^{**}=x_{ij,k}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The intuition is that the Krugman model 
\emph on
without
\emph default
 cross-industry markup heterogeneity is efficient; leaving no room for policy
 interventions to restore allocative efficiency.
