\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Third-Best: Unilaterally Optimal Import Tariffs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now suppose that, in addition to restrictions on industrial subsidies, the
 use of export subsidies is also restricted.
 The government's optimal policy problem in this case features two additional
 constraints, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{s}_{i}=\textbf{x}_{i}=\textbf{0}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)}\ \ \ W_{i}\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)\ \ \ \ \ s.t.\ \begin{cases}
\left(\textbf{t}_{i},\textbf{x}_{i},\textbf{s}_{i};\textbf{w}\right)\in\mathbb{F}\\
\textbf{s}_{i}=\textbf{x}_{i}=\textbf{0}
\end{cases}\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ (\textrm{P3}).
\]

\end_inset

Some variation of the above problem has been studied by an expansive literature
 on optimal tariffs.
 Though, nearly all existing studies are limited to partial equilibrium
 two-by-two models.
 Here, we use the same dual approach described earlier to analytically solve
 Problem (P3) within our multi-country, multi-industry general equilibrium
 framework.
 Our derivation, as before, yields simple sufficient statistics formulas
 for optimal third-best import taxes.
 The following theorem presents these formulas, with a formal proof provided
 in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: App Theorem 3"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In the special case where entry is restricted and countries are sufficiently
 small, the optimal tariff formula presented under Theorem 4 reduces to
 the formula used by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "lashkaripour2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to examine global tariff wars.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Suppose the government is unable to apply domestic industrial subsidies
 or export subsidies.
 In that case, 3rd-best optimal import tariffs are uniquely given by
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\textbf{1}+\textbf{t}_{ji}^{***}=\left(1+\bar{\tau}_{i}^{*}\right)(\textbf{1}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{ji})\oslash\left(\textbf{1}+\textbf{E}_{-ii}^{-1}\ \textbf{E}_{-ii}^{(ii)}\ \left[1-\frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+\overline{\mu}_{i}}\right]_{k}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\emph default
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\tau}_{i}^{*}=\left[-\sum_{g,s}\sum_{j\neq i}\chi_{ij,g}\left(1+\varepsilon_{ij,g}^{(ij,s)}\right)\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is a uniform tariff shifter that represents the elasticity of international
 demand for country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's labor (with 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{ij,g}\equiv P_{ij,g}Q_{ij,g}/\sum_{n\neq i}\textbf{P}_{in}\cdot\textbf{Q}_{in}$
\end_inset


\emph default
 denoting export shares).
 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\mu}_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the output-weighted average markup in economy 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 as described by Eq.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: profist margin"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

; and
\emph on
 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{-ii}$
\end_inset


\emph default
 and 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{E}_{-ii}^{(ii)}$
\end_inset


\emph default
 are matrixes of Marshallian demand elasticities as defined under Definition
 (D1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike Theorems 1 and 2, the third-best optimal tariff schedule identified
 by Theorem 3 is unique.
 That is because the multiplicity implied by Lemma 1 no longer applies when
 both export and industrial subsidies are restricted to zero.
 Nevertheless, the third-best tariff specified by Theorem 3 differs from
 the second-best tariffs (in Theorem 2) by only a uniform tariff shifter,
 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\bar{\tau}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 So, barring the uniform component, 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\bar{\tau}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we can understand the above formula based on the same intuition provided
 under Theorem 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The uniform tariff component, 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\bar{\tau}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, compensates for the unavailability of export tax-cum-subsidies to the
 government.
 By the Lerner symmetry, which is implicit in Lemma 1, import taxes can
 perfectly mimic a uniform export tax.
 This ability was previously redundant (under Theorems 1 and 2) because
 export taxes/subsidies were directly applicable, and there was no point
 in using other instruments to mimic them.
 But since export taxes are restricted under Problem (P3), it is optimal
 to uniformly raise all tariffs by a factor 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\bar{\tau}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, using them as a second-best substitute for optimal export taxes/subsidies.
\end_layout

