\end_inset

Tension between
\emph on
 
\emph default
Allocative Efficiency and ToT.
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Theorems 2 and 3 reveal that second-best trade policies seek to strike a
 balance between 
\begin_inset Formula $(a)$
\end_inset

 improving the terms-of-trade (ToT), which requires contracting exports
 in nationally differentiated (low-
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

) industries, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(b)$
\end_inset

 correcting misallocation, which requires expanding output in high-returns-to-sc
ale (high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

) industries.
 Obtaining this balance becomes difficult if not impossible when 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)<0$
\end_inset

—which is the empirically-relevant case based on our forthcoming estimation.
 To navigate this tension, a welfare-maximizing government must tailor its
 (2nd-best) trade policy in a way that curtails the ToT gains without necessaril
y correcting misallocation.
 This balancing act erodes the gains from 2nd-best trade policies and can
 even render them industry-blind—unable to beneficially correct inter-industry
 misallocation 
\emph on
or 
\emph default
manipulate industry-specific export market power.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In the canonical 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Krugman1980"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=1/\left(\sigma_{k}-1\right)$
\end_inset

, optimal (2nd-best) import tariffs and export subsidies are 
\emph on
industry-blind
\emph default
 for a small open economy.
 In particular, applying Theorem 2 to this special case yields,
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
vspace{-0.05in}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1+t_{ji,k}^{**}=1+\bar{t}_{i};\qquad\qquad\qquad1+x_{ij,k}^{**}=\left(1+\bar{t}_{i}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{\overline{\sigma}_{i}}\right),\vspace{-0.05in}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{t}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is an arbitrary tax shifter and 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\sigma}_{i}=\left(\sum_{k}\rho_{i,k}/\sigma_{k}\right)^{-1}$
\end_inset

 is the sales-weighted average trade elasticity facing country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 The optimal trade tax in each industry is evidently blind to misallocation
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

) or industry-specific export market power (
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

)—reflecting the difficulty to reconcile these two policy considerations.
 All this policy choice can achieve is to improve country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's aggregate ToT by inflating its wage relative to the rest of the world.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The tension between allocative efficiency and ToT can be theoretically demonstra
ted for a local change in policy (see online Appendix 
