\begin_layout Standard
The flip side of the noted tension is that if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)<0$
\end_inset

, unilateral implementation of scale-correcting Pigouvian subsidies worsens
 the ToT, resulting in possibly adverse welfare consequences.
 These arguments echo the 
\emph on
immiserizing growth
\emph default
 paradox in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "bhagwati1958immiserizing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and follow a simple logic.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)<0$
\end_inset

, scale-correcting Pigouvian subsidies expand domestic output in high-
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 industries, which are nationally differentiated industries in which countries
 hold significant export market power.
 Elevating output and, thus, exports in these industries could worsen the
 ToT to the point of triggering immiserizing welfare effects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Proof-of-Propositions 1 & 2"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrates theoretically that if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)<0$
\end_inset

, unilateral scale/markup correction worsens the ToT.
 But whether these adverse ToT effects outweigh the allocative efficiency
 gains from scale/markup correction is an empirical matter.
 We conjecture that if industry-level trade and scale elasticities exhibit
 a 
\emph on
strong
\emph default
 negative correlation, the adverse ToT effects from scale correction are
 large enough to cause immiserizing growth.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Conjecture
If industry-level trade and scale elasticities exhibit a strong negative
 correlation (
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{{Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)\ll0}}$
\end_inset

), unilateral scale (or markup) correction via industrial policy will likely
 worsen national welfare, echoing the immiserizing growth paradox.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We evaluate this conjecture with micro-estimated parameters in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Quantifying-the-Gains"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, but a numerical illustration with artificial parameter values is also
 provided in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig: Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 (right panel).
 This figure is produced by fixing the degree of inter-industry misallocation
 and artificially adjusting trade elasticities to vary 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)$
\end_inset

 from positive to negative values.
 In regions where 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)<0$
\end_inset

, a unilateral adoption of scale-correcting subsidies compromises welfare,
 causing immiserizing growth.
 These immiserizing consequences, as we discuss next, can be an obstacle
 for industrial policy implementation for countries committed to efficient
 trade policies under shallow agreements.
\emph on

