Immizerising growth can be a major obstacle to industrial policy implementation
 in open economies.
 To convey this point, we adopt the common view that intentional negotiations
 involve two stages.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Modeling international cooperation as a two-stage game consisting of 
\emph on
enactment
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
implementation
\emph default
 stages is commonplace in the global governance literature (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "shaffer2009hard"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 In the trade and environmental policy literature, many studies treat internatio
nal negotiations as multi-stage games where initial stages restrict policy
 choices and latter stages ensure implementation (e.g., 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "murdoch2003participation,drazen2008bargaining,kosfeld2009institution"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 In the first-stage, governments negotiate over policy space to ensure each
 party restricts itself to the globally efficient policy choice (Equation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: deep cooperation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 In the second stage, governments negotiate a deeper agreement to ensure
 the implementation of efficient (misallocation-correcting) policies, with
 each country having the choice to either implement efficient policies or
 withhold implementation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab: Coordination Game"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 illustrates the second-stage implementation game facing cooperative countries.
 The game involves two cooperative countries (
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

) that can take two actions: 
\begin_inset Formula $(1)$
\end_inset

 implement scale-correcting Pigouvian subsidies, 
\emph on
i.e.
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{s}_{i}=\boldsymbol{\mu}$
\end_inset

, or 
\begin_inset Formula $(2)$
\end_inset

 withhold implementation and stick to business as usual, 
\emph on
i.e.
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\textbf{s}_{i}=\textbf{0}$
\end_inset

.
 The efficient outcome is the implementation of Pigouvian subsidies in both
 countries, which will boost welfare by 2.7% across the board.
 But this outcome is not sustainable without formal coordination, because
 each country has an incentive to free-ride on the other country's implementatio
n.
 The outcome of this game is a race to bottom, wherein no party is willing
 to correct misallocation in domestic industries without violating its commitmen
ts to shallow cooperation (i.e., zero trade taxes).
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We must emphasize that a race to the bottom will not occur if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 In that case, a unilateral adoption of corrective subsidies improves rather
 than deteriorates the terms of trade, making unilateral implementation
 a dominant strategy.
 We nevertheless maintain our focus on the case where 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})<0$
\end_inset

 because it aligns with our forthcoming estimation of scale and trade elasticiti
es.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 A deep agreement that ensures reciprocity in industrial policy implementation
 can remedy this problem.
\end_layout

