Extensions and Application to other Canonical Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section, we first show that our theoretical results readily apply
 to two other canonical trade models.
 We then extend our baseline theoretical results to richer environments
 featuring input-output linkages and political economy pressures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Application to Other Canonical Trade Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The optimal policy formulas specified by Theorems 1-3 apply to two other
 canonical trade models.
 Though, parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 in these formulas adopt different interpretations, which reflects the different
 micro-foundation underlying these frameworks.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
The Eaton-Kortum model with Marshallian externalities.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
Consider a multi-industry 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Eaton2002"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model where industry-level production is subject to agglomeration economies.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{k}$
\end_inset

 denote the constant agglomeration elasticity in industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{k}$
\end_inset

 denote the Eaton-Kortum Fréchet shape parameter.
 Theorem 1 characterizes the optimal policy in this model under the following
 reinterpretation of parameters: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu{}_{k}^{\textrm{EK}}=\psi_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}^{\textrm{EK}}=1+\theta_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 The tension between the 
\emph on
ToT 
\emph default
and 
\emph on
allocative efficiency
\emph default
 manifests itself in this model if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\psi_{k},\theta_{k})<0$
\end_inset

.
 The fact that our theory readily extends to the Eaton-Kortum model echoes
 the isomorphism established in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kucheryavyy2023grounded"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Nested-EK"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 shows that this isomorphism is even more profound.
 The nested-CES import demand function implied by (A1), we demonstrate,
 may analogously arise from within-industry specialization 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
'a la
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Eaton-Kortum.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
The Melitz-Pareto model.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
Consider a multi-industry 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Melitz2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model that features the same nested-CES demand function specified by (A1).
 Suppose the firm-level productivity distribution is Pareto in each industry
 with a shape parameter, 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 Online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Appendix Firm-Selection"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 establishes that the Melitz-Pareto model is isomorphic to our baseline
 Krugman model insofar as macro-level representation is concerned.
 Hence, Theorem 1 characterizes the optimal policy in the Melitz-Pareto
 model under the following reinterpretation of parameters: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}^{\textrm{Melitz}}=\frac{\gamma_{k}\vartheta_{k}}{\left(\gamma_{k}-1\right)\left(\vartheta_{k}+1\right)-\vartheta_{k}}-1$
\end_inset

 if entry is restricted and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}^{\textrm{Melitz}}=\frac{1}{\vartheta_{k}}$
\end_inset

 if entry is free; and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}^{\textrm{Melitz}}=1+\vartheta_{k}\left[1+\vartheta_{k}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma_{k}-1}-\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}\right)\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 This mapping indicates that we need to estimate parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta_{k}$
\end_inset

, in addition to 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}$
\end_inset

, to quantify the gains from policy under firm-selection effects—a procedure
 we formally undertake and elaborate on in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Quantifying-the-Gains"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Extension #1:
\emph on
 
\emph default
Accounting for Input-Output Networks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose production employs both labor and intermediate inputs, which are
 distinguished from final goods by superscript 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

.
 Cost minimization entails that the producer price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $ij,k$
\end_inset

 (origin 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

–destination 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

) depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 the wage rate in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

 the price of all intermediate inputs, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}^{\mathcal{I}}\equiv\{\tilde{P}_{nj,k}^{\mathcal{I}}\}$
\end_inset

, available to firms in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Namely,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{ij,k}=\bar{\rho}_{ij,k}\textrm{C}_{i,k}(w_{i},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}^{\mathcal{I}})\mathcal{Q}_{i,k}^{-\frac{\mu_{k}}{1+\mu_{k}}},\label{eq: Producer Price (IO)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\textrm{C}_{i,k}(.)$
\end_inset

 is a homogeneous of degree one function w.r.t.
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i}^{\mathcal{I}}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Without loss of generality, we assume that good 
\begin_inset Formula $ji,k$
\end_inset

 can be used as either an intermediate input or a final consumption good,
 with taxes being applied on a good irrespective of the intended final use,
 i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{P}_{ij,k}^{\mathcal{I}}=\tilde{P}_{ij,k}$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption is innocuous, because we can fragment every industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 into a final good version 
\begin_inset Formula $k'$
\end_inset

 and an intermediate good version 
\begin_inset Formula $k''$
\end_inset

.
 Since we do not impose any restrictions on the number of industries, our
 theory extends to the case where differential taxes are imposed on fragments
 
\begin_inset Formula $k'$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k''$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 The dependence of 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{ij,k}$
\end_inset

 on gross industry-level output, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Q}_{i,k}\equiv\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}\left[\bar{a}_{ij,k}Q_{ij,k}\right]$
\end_inset

, reflects scale economies under free entry.
 The formal definition of general equilibrium under input-output (IO) linkages
 is presented in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Extended Optimal-Tax-Formula"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 The same appendix characterizes optimal policy using an augmented version
 of our dual approach that leverages additional supply-side envelope conditions.
 Our characterization reveals that the formulas for optimal industrial subsidies
 and import tariffs are IO-blind—
\emph on
i.e.
\emph default
, they are similar to Theorem 1.
 There is a simple intuition for this result: Although a tariff on imported
 inputs can influence prices in the rest of the world through re-exportation,
 any potential ToT gains from tariff re-exportation are already internalized
 by the government's optimal choice vis-
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
`a
\end_layout

\end_inset

-vis export tax-cum-subsidies.
 Consistent with this intuition, the optimal export subsidy formula features
 an explicit adjustment for export subsidy reimportation via the IO network.
 The following theorem formalizes these results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Under IO linkages, the unilaterally first-best import tariffs and domestic
 subsidies are IO-blind; but 1st-best export subsidies exhibit an upward-adjustm
ent that account for re-importation via the IO network.
 More formally, the unilaterally first-best policy schedule is given by
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\hspace{-0.25in}[\text{domestic subsidy}]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1+s_{i,k}^{*} & =\left(1+\mu_{k}\right)(1+\bar{s}_{i}^{\mathscr{C}})^{-1}\\
\hspace{-0.25in}[\text{import tax}]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1+t_{ji,k}^{*} & =(1+\omega_{ji,k})\left(1+\bar{t}_{i}\right)(1+\bar{s}_{i}^{\mathscr{C}})\\
\hspace{-0.25in}[\text{export subsidy}]\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \textbf{1}+\textbf{x}_{ij}^{*} & =-\textbf{E}_{ij}^{-1}\left[\textbf{E}_{ij}^{(-ij)}\left(\textbf{1}+\textbf{t}_{i}^{*}\right)+\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{ij}(1+\bar{t}_{i})(1+\bar{s}_{i}^{\mathscr{C}})\right],
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\emph default
where elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{ij}\equiv\left[\Lambda_{ij,k}\right]_{k}$
\end_inset

 correspond to the fraction of good 
\begin_inset Formula $ij,k$
\end_inset

 that is reimported, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{s}_{i}^{\mathscr{C}}$
\end_inset

is an arbitrary tax shifter that assumes a positive value if the taxed item
 is a final good and zero otherwise.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is a small open economy, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda_{ij,k}\approx\boldsymbol{0}$
\end_inset

.
 Correspondingly, optimal policy formulas for a small open economy under
 IO linkages perfectly overlap with the baseline formulas specified under
 Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: small open economy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above theorem indicates that the tax-equivalent of export subsidies
 is relatively lower on intermediate inputs to mitigate export tax-reimportation
 via the IO network.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In the absence of scale economies or profits, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}=0$
\end_inset

.
 So, Theorem 1 posits that optimal import tariffs are uniform by choice
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{s}_{i}^{\mathscr{C}}=0$
\end_inset

 in the multi-industry Armington and Eaton–Kortum models (see online Appendix
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Simple IO"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Moreover, there is a uniform wedge between final and intermediate input
 taxes as represented by the final good tax-shifter, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{s}_{i}^{\mathscr{C}}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Theorem 4 indicates that uniform markup pricing is not a necessary condition
 for efficiency.
 Consider, for instance, a vertical production economy where goods are used
 as either final goods or intermediate inputs, but not both.
 The efficient policy, in this setup, must restore uniform markup pricing
 within input and final good segments but not across—see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "antras2022trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for further exploration of this issue.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This detail aside, the ToT-improving motive for policy still requires export
 contraction in low-
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 industries, while the misallocation-correcting objective asks for output
 expansion in high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 industries.
 So, unless intermediate inputs exhibit a systemically lower 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

, our conjecture about the inefficacy of standalone trade policy measures
 withstands.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Theorem 4 offers insight into the structure of second-best import tariffs
 and export subsidies under IO linkages.
 Several papers, including 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "blanchard2016global"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "beshkar2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "caliendo2021second"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "antras2022trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, examine in more detail how IO linkages impact 3rd-best import tariff choices.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "beshkar2020cost"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, moreover, adopt a special case of Theorem 4, with Cobb-Douglas production
 and no scale economies, to examine the cost of trade wars.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Extension #2:
\emph on
 
\emph default
Accounting for Political Economy Pressures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose optimal policy choices internalize political economy pressures 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
`a la
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "ossa2014trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

's 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeyearpar
key "ossa2014trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 adaptation of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "grossman1994protection"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 That is, the government maximizes a politically-weighted welfare function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{i}\equiv V_{i}(w_{i}L_{i}+\mathcal{R}_{i}+\sum_{k}\pi_{i,k}\Pi_{i,k},\tilde{\textbf{P}}_{i})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 is the political economy weight assigned to industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

's profits (with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k}\pi_{i,k}/K=$
\end_inset

1).
 It follows trivially from Theorem 1 that the first-best policy in the political
 setup consists of the same trade tax/subsidy formulas but a politically-adjuste
d industrial subsidy rate.
 Namely,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
1+s_{i,k}^{*} & =(1+\mu_{i,k}^{\mathscr{P}})\left(1+\bar{s}_{i}\right),
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{i,k}^{\mathscr{P}}=\frac{\mu_{k}}{\pi_{i,k}-(1-\pi_{i,k})\mu_{k}}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
political economy-adjusted
\emph default
 markup of industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
 Considering the above formulas: if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\pi_{i,k},\mu_{k}\right)<0$
\end_inset

, the optimal policy may tax high-
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 industries to the detriment of social welfare.
 In that case, even if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})>0$
\end_inset

, the misallocation-correcting and ToT motives for trade taxation will clash.
 However, if 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\pi_{i,k},\mu_{k}\right)\geq0$
\end_inset

, our conjecture about the inefficacy of standalone trade policy measures
 withstands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Estimation"

\end_inset

Estimating the Key Policy Parameters
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on our theory, policy evaluation in open economies requires credible
 estimates for industry-level 
\emph on
trade elasticities
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

, and industry-level 
\emph on
scale elasticities
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}\sim\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}$
\end_inset

.
 The former governs the degree of national-level market power, while the
 latter regulates the extent of love-for-variety.
 The trade literature has paid considerable attention to estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

, but less to estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 The existing policy literature typically normalizes 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 in one of two ways: 
\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{k}-1}$
\end_inset

 in imperfectly competitive models, and 
\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=0$
\end_inset

 in perfectly competitive models.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ossa2016quantitative"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "costinot2014trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a synthesis of the previous literature.
 Under restricted entry, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 denotes the firm-level markup, which can be alternatively estimated with
 firm-level production data (e.g., 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "de2012markups"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "deloecker16"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Both normalizations impose artificial restrictions on 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\mu_{k},\sigma_{k}\right)$
\end_inset

, which following the discussion in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, can bias the estimated gains from policy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Against this backdrop, we seek to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 in a way that ascertains mutual consistency, giving us a credible assessment
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\mu_{k},\sigma_{k}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 To this end, we propose a new methodology that simultaneously estimates
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 from the same data.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In the presence of firm-selection effects, our estimated parameters are
 necessary but not sufficient to pin down the trade and scale elasticities—see
 online Appendixes 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Appendix Firm-Selection"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Gains Robustness"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for details.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Our approach involves fitting the structural 
\emph on
firm-level
\emph default
 import demand function implied by A1 to the universe of Colombian import
 transactions from 2007–2013.
 We outline this approach below, starting with a description of the data
 used in our estimation.
\emph on

