\begin_layout Subsection
Estimating Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since we are focusing on one importer, we hereafter drop the importer's
 subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and add a year subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to account for the time dimension of our data.
 With this switch in notation, the demand facing firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 located in country 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 and supplying product 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 in year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
q_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)=\varphi_{j,kt}(\omega)\left(\frac{\tilde{{p}}_{j,kt}(\omega)}{\tilde{P}_{j,kt}}\right)^{-\gamma_{k}}\left(\frac{\tilde{P}_{j,kt}}{\tilde{P}_{kt}}\right)^{-\sigma_{k}}Q_{kt},\label{eq: Import Demand Function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, in our theoretical model, designated industries.
 In our estimation, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 denotes an HS10 product—the most disaggregated product classification in
 our data.
 The quadruplet 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\omega jkt$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 accordingly denotes a unique variety corresponding to 
\emph on
firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

–country of origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

–HS10 product 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

–year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset


\emph default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}(\omega)\equiv\tilde{p}(\omega)q(\omega)$
\end_inset

 denote gross sales.
 Rearranging Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Import Demand Function"

\end_inset

 yields the following log-linear import demand function facing individual
 varieties:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ln\tilde{x}_{j,kt}(\omega)=\left(1-\sigma_{k}\right)\ln\tilde{p}_{j,kt}(\omega)+\left(1-\frac{\sigma_{k}-1}{\gamma_{k}-1}\right)\ln\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)+D_{kt}+\ln\varphi_{j,kt}(\omega),\label{eq: Estimated Equation Final}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{kt}\equiv\ln P_{kt}^{\sigma_{k}}Q_{kt}$
\end_inset

 can be treated as a product-year fixed effect and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes the share of expenditure on firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
conditional
\emph default
 on buying good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 from country of origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)\equiv\varphi_{j,kt}(\omega)\left(\frac{\tilde{{p}}_{j,kt}(\omega)}{\tilde{P}_{j,kt}}\right)^{1-\gamma{}_{k}}=\frac{\tilde{x}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)}{\sum_{\omega'\in\Omega_{j,kt}}\tilde{x}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)}.
\]

\end_inset

We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{jkt}(\omega)=\bar{\varphi}_{j,k}(\omega)\times\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

 can be decomposed into a time-invariant 
\emph on
firm-and-product
\emph default
-specific quality component, 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\varphi}_{j,k}(\omega)$
\end_inset

, and a time-varying component 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

, that encompasses idiosyncratic variations in consumer taste, measurement
 errors, and/or omitted variables that account for dynamic demand optimization.
 To eliminate 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\varphi}_{j,k}(\omega)$
\end_inset

 from the estimating equation, we employ a first-difference estimator, which
 also drops observations pertaining to one-time exporters.
 We deem the first-difference estimator appropriate given the possibility
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

's are sequentially correlated.
 As a robustness check, we also report estimation results based on a two-way
 fixed effects estimator in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Two-Ways FE Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "boehm2020long"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, the first-difference estimation offers a partial remedy for omitted variable
 bias and reverse causality.
 Both issues pose a serious challenge to traditional log-level estimations
 of import demand.
 Depending on the application, though, the first-difference estimator may
 not necessarily identify the desired long-run elasticity.
 As detailed in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Two-Ways FE Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, this limitation is less severe in our firm-level estimation—as we explicitly
 control for the extensive margin of trade.
 We illustrate this point formally in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Two-Ways FE Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 by re-estimating Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Estimated Equation Final"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 in levels and comparing the estimation results to the baseline values.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Stated in terms of first-differences, our estimating equation takes the
 following form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Delta\ln\tilde{x}_{j,kt}(\omega)=\left(1-\sigma_{k}\right)\Delta\ln\tilde{p}{}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)+\left(1-\frac{\sigma_{k}-1}{\gamma_{k}-1}\right)\Delta\ln\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)+\Delta D_{kt}+\Delta\ln\varphi{}_{\omega jkt},\label{eq:Main}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

, roughly speaking, represents a variety-specific demand shock; and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta D_{kt}$
\end_inset

 is a product-year fixed effect.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
To handle outliers, we trims our sample to exclude observations that report
 a price change, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\tilde{p}{}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

, above the 99th percentile or below the 1st percentile of the HS10 product
 code 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 in year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Of the remaining variables, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\tilde{p}_{j,kt}(\omega)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\tilde{x}_{j,kt}(\omega)$
\end_inset

 are directly observable for each import variety.
 The change in the 
\emph on
within-national
\emph default
 market share, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

, can be calculated using the universe of firm-level sales to Colombia.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\emph on
Recovering Scale & Trade Elasticities from Demand Parameters.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 allows us to estimate demand parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}$
\end_inset

, from which we can recover the scale and trade elasticities as follows
 (see Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Theoretical-Framework"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 for the underlying theoretical foundation):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu_{k}=\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}\sim\text{scale elasticity}\qquad\qquad\qquad\text{\ensuremath{\sigma_{k}}}\sim\text{trade elasticity}
\]

\end_inset

The reason we can infer 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 from demand parameters is that the scale elasticity in the generalized
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "Krugman1980"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 model reflects the extent of 
\emph on
love-for-variety
\emph default
—the social benefits of which are not internalized by firms' entry decisions.
 In the 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citeauthor
key "Melitz2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

-Pareto case, we also need estimates for the shape of the Pareto productivity
 distribution (in addition to 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

) to recover the scale elasticity—see online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Robustness-Checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 Our estimation is, of course, unable to detect scale externalities unrelated
 to love-for-variety.
 These externalities can be estimated using techniques that leverage scale-relat
ed moments, but under stronger assumptions about the variability of production
 inputs.
 We discuss the relative advantages of each technique in online Appendix
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Overview-of-Scale-Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In the restricted entry case, our demand estimation identifies the firm-level
 markup in each industry.
 Following an old tradition in the industrial organization literature, we
 assume that market conduct is monopolistic competition and recover firm-level
 markups as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=\frac{1}{\gamma_{k}-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Appendix Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 recovers markups under alternative market conduct assumptions.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\emph on
Breaking the Sample into Broadly-Defined Industries.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
As noted earlier, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 indexes an HS10 product category in Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 To conduct our forthcoming quantitative analysis, we must estimate demand
 parameters for 14 broadly-defined industries based on the World Input-Output
 Database (WIOD) industry classification.
 Considering this, we pool all HS10 products belonging to the same WIOD
 industry 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{K}$
\end_inset

 together and estimate Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 on this pooled sample assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}$
\end_inset

 are uniform across products within the same industry (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{k}=\gamma_{\mathcal{K}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}=\sigma_{\mathcal{K}}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in\mathbb{K}_{\mathcal{K}}$
\end_inset

).
 In principle, we can also estimate the import demand function separately
 for each HS10 product category to attain HS10-level elasticities.
 However, such elasticities will be of little use for quantitative policy
 analysis, as multi-country data on trade, production, and expenditure shares
 are scarce at such levels of disaggregation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Identification Strategy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The identification challenge we face is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\tilde{p}{}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 are endogenous variables that can covary with the demand shock, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\varphi{}_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Another challenge is that unit price data may be contaminated with measurement
 errors, as they are averaged across many transactions.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Berry1994"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, this type of measurement error is fairly innocuous when dealing with log-linea
r demand functions.
 Furthermore, our instrumental variable approach will handle measurement
 errors, provided that lagged monthly sales patterns are uncorrelated with
 concurrent measurement errors.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Traditional 
\emph on
country-level
\emph default
 import demand estimations overcome a similar challenge by instrumenting
 for prices with plausibly exogenous tariff rates.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A prominent example is 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "caliendo2014estimates"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, who use tariff data to identify the trade elasticity.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset

 This strategy, however, does not suit our 
\emph on
firm-level
\emph default
 estimation because tariffs discriminate by country-of-origin but not across
 firms from the same country.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We employ a shift-share research design to overcome our 
\emph on
firm-level
\emph default
 identification challenge.
 Our approach is rooted in two data observations: First, a typical variety
 is imported under multiple invoices spread across different months in a
 given year.
 As a matter of accounting, the annual-level price of a variety is the quantity-
weighted average of monthly prices.
 Namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\tilde{s}_{j,kt}(\omega;m)p_{j,kt}\left(\omega;m\right)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 denotes month and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{s}\left(\omega;m\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\omega;m\right)$
\end_inset

 denote the quantity share and price associated with month 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Second, the month 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 price of an imported variety (in Colombian Pesos) is equal to 
\emph on
markup-plus-taxe
\emph default
s
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset


\emph on
marginal input cost invoiced in local currency
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

exchange rate in month 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 
\emph default
More formally, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{j,kt}\left(\omega;m\right)=\tau_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)\times\text{C}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)\times\mathcal{E}_{jt}(m)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{C}$
\end_inset

 respectively denote markup-plus-tax and marginal cost, while 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{E}_{jt}(m)$
\end_inset

 represents the exchange rate between origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

's currency and the Colombian Peso in month 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 of year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 To a first-order approximation, the annual change in variety-level prices
 in response to monthly exchange rate fluctuations can be specified as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta\ln\tilde{p}{}_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)\approx\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}s_{j,kt}(\omega;m)\,\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}(m),
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}(m)$
\end_inset

 denotes the year-over-year change in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

's exchange rate with the Colombian Peso in month 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 ; and 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{j,kt}(\omega;m)$
\end_inset

 is the share of month 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 in variety 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega jkt$
\end_inset

's annual export sales to Colombia:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{j,kt}\left(\omega;m\right)=\frac{\tilde{x}_{j,kt}(\omega;m)}{\sum_{m'\in\mathcal{M}}\tilde{x}_{j,kt}(\omega;m')}\,\sim\,\text{share of month \ensuremath{m} in annual export sales}
\]

\end_inset

Capitalizing on the above observation, we construct our shift-share instrument
 as the inner product of 
\emph on
lagged
\emph default
 monthly export shares and monthly exchange rate changes.
 Namely,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{j,kt}(\omega)=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}s_{j,kt-1}\left(\omega,m\right)\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}\left(m\right).
\]

\end_inset

Stated verbally, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j,kt}(\omega)$
\end_inset

 measures exposure to exchange rate fluctuations at the 
\emph on
firm
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

origin
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

product
\begin_inset Formula $\times$
\end_inset

year
\emph default
 level.
 The idea being that aggregate exchange rate movements have differential
 effects on individual firms depending on the monthly composition of their
 prior export activity to Colombia.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Leveraging exchange rate data to construct firm-level input cost shifters
 has been also explored by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "piveteau2019estimating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "amiti2019international"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Encouragingly, the validity of our instrument is corroborated by the strong
 and statistically significant correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\tilde{p}$
\end_inset

.
 Online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Illustrative-Example"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 illustrates this relationship visually using two U.S.-based firms as an example.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Exclusion Restriction.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Our instrument utilizes lagged export shares, which depend on lagged prices
 and a set of market-level indexes—namely, 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{jkt-1}(\omega,m)=s(\tilde{p}_{jkt-1}(\omega,m);...)$
\end_inset

.
 Considering this, the exclusion restriction in our setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[z\,\Delta\ln\varphi\right]=0$
\end_inset

, rests on two conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Labeling
\labelwidthstring 00.00.0000
(C1) Prior price-setting decisions (and thus lagged export shares) are orthogona
l to concurrent demand shocks: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\tilde{p}_{j,kt-1}(\omega)\,\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Labeling
\labelwidthstring 00.00.0000
(C2) Monthly national-level exchange rate shocks are orthogonal to variety-level
 demand shocks: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}(m)\,\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}\right]=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since our sample features many firms and a finite number of months, Condition
 (C1) is sufficient for the consistency of our estimates (see Proposition
 2.1 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citealp
key "goldsmith2020bartik"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 In fact, our two-stage least squares (2SLS) estimator is numerically equivalent
 to a generalized method of moments (GMM) estimator with lagged monthly
 export shares as instruments and a weight matrix constructed from monthly
 aggregate exchange rate shocks.
 Condition (C2), meanwhile, is more crucial for the finite sample properties
 of our estimator.
 Both conditions can, in principle, be violated if there are cross-inventory
 linkages or if individual export varieties account for a significant fraction
 of national exports to Colombia.
 We discuss and address these issues in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec: Discussion of Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Khandelwal2010"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, we construct two standard instruments for the annual variation in the
 within-national market shares: 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $(i)$
\end_inset


\emph default
 annual changes in the total number of origin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 firms serving the Colombian market in product category 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, and 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $(ii)$
\end_inset


\emph default
 changes in the total number of HS10 product categories actively served
 by firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 in year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 These count measures will be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\lambda_{j,kt}\left(\omega\right)$
\end_inset

 but uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

 if variety-level entry and exit occurs prior to, or independent of, the
 demand shock realization of competing varieties.
 As noted by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Khandelwal2010"
literal "true"

\end_inset

, this assumption is widely-invoked when estimating discrete choice demands
 curves—see also 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Berry1995"
literal "true"

\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Border taxes tend to be a weak instrument for firm-level prices in our sample,
 but we include to comply with the past literature.
 These include applied ad-valorem tariffs and the Columbian value-added
 tax (VAT).
 We exclude the VAT component in the 'Transportation' and 'Petroleum' industries
 since the VAT in these industries discriminates by the delivery method
 and level of luxury—both of which may be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Estimation Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab: Estimation result by sector"

\end_inset

 reports our industry-level estimation results.
 We also report results corresponding to a pooled sample of all industries
 in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:Estimation results"

\end_inset

 of the online appendix.
 The same table compares the 2SLS and OLS estimates to ensure that our IV
 strategy operates in the expected direction.
 Our estimates point to a median trade elasticity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma-1=3.9$
\end_inset

 and a median scale elasticity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\approx0.20$
\end_inset

.
 Our pooled estimation yields a heteroskedasticity-robust Kleibergen-Paap
 Wald rk F-statistic of 
\begin_inset Formula $259$
\end_inset

, rejecting the null of weak instruments given the Stock-Yogo critical values.
 A similar albeit weaker outcome emerges from the industry-level estimation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The industry-level elasticities reported in Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab: Estimation result by sector"

\end_inset

 display considerable variation across industries.
 The estimated scale elasticity or markup margin is highest in the 'Electrical
 & Optical Equipment' (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=0.55$
\end_inset

) and 'Petroleum' (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=1.2$
\end_inset

) industries; both of which are associated with high R&D or fixed costs.
 The estimated scale elasticity is lowest in 'Agricultural & Mining' (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=0.14)$
\end_inset

 and 'Machinery' (
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=0.12$
\end_inset

) industries.
 Furthermore, with the exception of 'Agriculture & Mining,' we cannot reject
 the prevalence of scale economies due to 
\emph on
love-for-variety
\emph default
.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The finding that returns-to-scale are negligible in the agricultural sector
 aligns with a large body of evidence on the inverse farm-size productivity
 (IFSP) relationship—see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "sen1962aspect"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and subsequent references to IFSP.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Importantly, our estimates indicate that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\sigma_{k}-1}{\gamma_{k}-1}\neq1$
\end_inset

 in nearly all industries.
 This finding rejects the arbitrary link often assumed between the firm-level
 and national-level degrees of market power in the literature.
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In other words, our estimation rejects the 
\emph on
independence of irrelevant alternatives
\emph default
 (IIA) because product varieties or technologies are less differentiated
 intra-nationally than inter-nationally.
 While the IIA assumption has garnered considerable attention in the industrial
 organization literature, the trade literature has only recently tested
 this assumption against data.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "redding2016uni"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 estimate an international demand system that relaxes the IIA assumption
 by accommodating heterogeneous taste across consumers.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "adao2015nonparametric"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 estimate a trade model that (unlike standard CES models) permits varieties
 from certain countries to be closer substitutes.
 Our results contribute to this emerging literature by highlighting another
 aspect of the trade data that is at odds with the IIA assumption.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 Our estimates also indicate that
\size large

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})\,\approx\,-0.65,
\]

\end_inset


