\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec: Discussion of Estimation"

\end_inset

Challenges to Identification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our two conditions for identification, (C1) and (C2), can be contested under
 certain circumstances.
 Below, we discuss these issues and present additional evidence to address
 them.
\begin_inset VSpace smallskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Within-Cluster Correlation in Error Terms.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "adao2019shift"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 show that identification based on shift-share instruments exhibits an over-reje
ction problem if regression errors are cross-correlated.
 In the context of our estimation, this problem will arise if demand shocks
 are correlated across 
\emph on
firm-origin-product-year
\emph default
 varieties with a similar monthly export composition.
 We adopt a conservative two-way clustering of standard errors by 
\emph on
product-year 
\emph default
and 
\emph on
origin-product
\emph default
 to handle this issue.
 Clustering standard errors in this manner is akin to the correction proposed
 by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "adao2019shift"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Dynamic Cross-Inventory Effects.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Lagged inventory clearances can challenge our identifying assumptions on
 two fronts.
 First, firms' optimal pricing decisions may be forward-looking, violating
 Condition (C1).
 To address this concern, we reconstruct our shift-share instrument using
 four lags instead of one.
 If inventories clear in at most four years, we can deduce that pricing
 decisions do not internalize expected demand shocks beyond the four-year
 mark.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\tilde{p}_{jkt-4}(\omega)\,\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}\right]=0$
\end_inset

, and the new instrument will satisfy the exclusion restriction.
 The trade-off is that we lose observations, as the instrument is constructible
 for only firms that continuously export in the 4 different years.
 The 
\emph on
top
\emph default
 panel of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Robustness-checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 (in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Robustness-Checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) compares estimation results under this alternative instrument to the baseline
 results.
 The new estimation preserves the ordering and magnitude of our estimated
 elasticities, retaining the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)$
\end_inset

, which is pivotal for optimal policy outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, with cross-inventory linkages, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}$
\end_inset

 may encompass omitted variables that reflect firms' dynamic inventory managemen
t decisions.
 One of these omitted variables is presumably the exchange rate.
 If so, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}(m)\,\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}\right]\neq0$
\end_inset

, which violates Condition (C2).
 To address this concern, we reestimate Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 while directly controlling for the annual change in the exchange rate,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}$
\end_inset

.
 Even if changes in inventory-related demand depend on the changes in the
 exchange rate, we can still assert that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[z_{j,kt}(\omega)\,\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}\mid\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}\right]=0$
\end_inset

—i.e., the exclusions restriction is satisfied with the added control, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\ln\mathcal{E}_{jt}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\emph on
middle
\emph default
 panel of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Robustness-checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 (in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Robustness-Checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) compares estimation results from this alternative specification to the
 baseline results.
 Reassuringly, The new estimation preserves the ordering and magnitude of
 our estimated elasticities and the negative correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Export Varieties with Significant Market Share.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Our identification can come under threat if individual varieties account
 for a significant fraction of a country's sales to Colombia.
 In such a case, variety-specific demand shocks can influence the bilateral
 exchange between the Colombian Peso and the origin country's currency,
 thereby violating Condition (C2).
 This concern, however, does not apply to our sample of exporters.
 The variety with the highest 99th percentile within-national market share
 accounts for only 0.1% of the origin country's total exports to Colombia.
 The variety with the highest 90th percentile within-national market share
 accounts for only 0.0008% of the origin country's total exports to Colombia.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One may remain concerned about large multi-product firms that export multiple
 product varieties to Colombia in a given year.
 Consider, for instance, a multi-product firm 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

 that exports goods 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 to Colombia in year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 If demand shocks are correlated across varieties supplied by this firm
 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jkt}\,\Delta\ln\varphi_{\omega jgt}\right]\neq0$
\end_inset

), Condition (C2) may be violated despite each variety's market share being
 infinitesimally small.
 To address this issue, we reestimate Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Main"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 on a restricted sample that drops excessively large firms with a total
 within-national market share that exceeds 0.1%.
 The 
\emph on
bottom
\emph default
 panel of Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Robustness-checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 (in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Robustness-Checks"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) compares estimation results from the trimmed sample to the baseline results.
 Encouragingly, the ordering and magnitude of the estimated elasticities
 are preserved across industries.
 The new estimation also retains the negative correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec: BCDR"

\end_inset

Plausibility of Estimates and Limitations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We conclude this section by discussing the plausibility of our estimates.
 We do so by exploring their macro-level implications and comparing them
 to counterparts in the literature.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\emph on
Plausibility from the Lens of Macro-Level Predictions.
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

Our scale elasticity estimates can be evaluated based on their prediction
 about the income-size elasticity.
 As pointed out by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ramondo2016trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, the factual relationship between real per capita income and population
 size (i.e., the income-size elasticity) is negative and statistically insignifica
nt.
 Quantitative trade models featuring the normalization 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu(\sigma-1)=\frac{\sigma-1}{\gamma-1}=1$
\end_inset

, however, predict a strong and positive income-size elasticity that remains
 significant even after the introduction of domestic trade frictions.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ramondo2016trade"
literal "true"

\end_inset

 call this observation the income-size elasticity puzzle.
 Considering this puzzle, in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Beyond-Policy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 we compute the income-size elasticity implied by our estimated value of
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\sigma-1}{\gamma-1}\approx0.67$
\end_inset

.
 Encouragingly, our estimated value for 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\sigma-1}{\gamma-1}$
\end_inset

 completely resolves the aforementioned puzzle.
 In other words, our micro-estimated elasticities are consistent with the
 macro-level cross-national relationship between population size and real
 per capita income.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\emph on
Comparison to Counterparts in the Literature.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset


\emph default
Reassuringly, our estimates align closely with well-known industry-level
 case studies.
 Take, for example, our elasticity estimates for the 'Petroleum' industry,
 which appear somewhat extreme.
 First, our estimate for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{k}$
\end_inset

 aligns with the consensus in the Energy Economics literature that national-leve
l demand for petroleum products is price-inelastic.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pesaran1998energy"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for specific estimates and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "fattouh2007drivers"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a survey of this literature.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Second, our estimated 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}$
\end_inset

 for the 'Petroleum' industry closely resembles existing estimates in the
 Industrial Organization literature.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "considine2001markup"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, for instance, estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\approx1.15$
\end_inset

 using detailed data on the U.S.
 petroleum industry.
 Moreover, our finding that the 'Petroleum' industry is the most scale-intensive
 industry is consistent with the finding in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "antweiler2002increasing"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, which is based on more aggregated data.
 Likewise, consider the 'Transportation' or auto industry, where our estimated
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{k}=0.13$
\end_inset

 implies an optimal markup of 13%.
 This estimate aligns with existing estimates from various industry-level
 studies.
 Recently, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cocsar2018drives"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 have estimated markups for the auto industry that range between roughly
 6% to 13%.
 Previously, 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Berry1995"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 have estimated markups of around 20% in the U.S.
 auto industry using data from 1971-1990.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\emph on
Limitation
\emph default
s
\emph on
 of our Estimation Technique
\emph default
.
\begin_inset Formula $\quad$
\end_inset

A potential limitation of our estimation technique is that we do not directly
 leverage scale-related moments in our estimation.
 The scale elasticity can be alternatively estimated using 
\emph on
direct
\emph default
 estimation techniques that leverage such moments, as reviewed in online
 Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Overview-of-Scale-Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 These techniques have the advantage of detecting scale externalities unrelated
 to 
\emph on
love-for-variety
\emph default
—albeit under additional assumptions about the variability of production
 inputs.
 While we are mindful of this limitation, we think our approach may be appropria
te for policy evaluation in certain settings as it separately identifies
 the trade elasticity from the scale elasticity, delivering credible estimates
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov\left(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

