\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec: Mapping Formulas to Data"

\end_inset

Mapping Optimal Policy Formulas to Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The formulas provided by Theorems 1-3 allow us to compute the 
\emph on
maximal
\emph default
 gains from policy without resorting to numerical optimization.
 This approach can be useful considering that numerical optimization routines
 (like 
\noun on
Matlab
\noun default
's 
\noun on
Fmincon
\noun default
) have well-known limitations when applied to non-linear models with many
 free-moving variables.
 Our 
\emph on
optimization-free
\emph default
 procedure 
\begin_inset Formula $(a)$
\end_inset

 specifies optimal tax/subsidy choices as a function of equilibrium variables
 (e.g., expenditure shares) using the formulas provided by Theorems 1-3, and
 
\begin_inset Formula $(b)$
\end_inset

 specifies equilibrium variables as a function of optimal tax/subsidy choices
 based on equilibrium constraints.
 The system of equations implied by 
\begin_inset Formula $(a)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(b)$
\end_inset

 is then jointly solved to compute welfare outcomes under optimal policy.
 Our method utilizes the exact hat-algebra technique whereby 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}=z^{*}/z$
\end_inset

 denotes the counterfactual change in a generic variable after implementing
 optimal policies.
 Our presentation here is focused on first-best policies under free entry.
 Online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Quantitative-Methodology FE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrates how the same approach can be applied to other optimal policy
 scenarios.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To map our theory to data, we must take a stance on the cross-industry utility
 aggregator, which we assume is Cobb-Douglas
\emph on
—i.e.
\emph default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{i}\left(\textbf{Q}_{i}\right)=\prod_{k}Q_{i,k}^{e_{i,k}}$
\end_inset

.
 Under this parametrization, we need data on observable shares, national
 accounts, and applied taxes, which we denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}=\left\{ \lambda_{ji,k},r_{ji,k},e_{i,k},\text{\ensuremath{\rho_{i,k}}},Y_{i},w_{i}L_{i},x_{ij,k},t_{ji,k},s_{i,k}\right\} _{j,i,k}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
As explained in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Theoretical-Framework"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, under free entry, the number of firms operating in 
\emph on
origin 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\emph default
 can be expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{i,k}=\bar{m}_{i,k}\rho_{i,k}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 is composed of parameters and variables that are invariant to policy.
 We can, therefore, use 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 to track scale economies that channel through entry—as detailed under Equation
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: Producer Price"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 We also need estimates for the industry-level scale and trade elasticities,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta=\left\{ \sigma_{k},\mu_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

, which were obtained in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Estimation"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As discussed under Theorem 1, country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's first-best optimal policy schedule in the Cobb-Douglas case is described
 by the following set of formulas:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1+s_{i,k}^{*}=1+\mu_{k};\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1+t_{ji,k}^{*}=1+\omega_{ji,k}^{*};\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ 1+x_{ij,k}^{*}=\frac{(\sigma_{k}-1)\sum_{n\neq i}\left[(1+\omega_{ni,g}^{*})\lambda_{nj,k}^{*}\right]}{1+(\sigma_{k}-1)(1-\lambda_{ij,k}^{*})};
\]

\end_inset

where superscript 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $*$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 indicates that a variable is evaluated in the counterfactual optimal policy
 equilibrium.
 Using the hat-algebra notation and our expression for the good-specific
 supply elasticity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

 (Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: XSE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

), we can write the above formulas in changes as follows:
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The multiplier 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi_{ji,k}^{*}=1-\left(1-\frac{1}{\mu_{k}}\right)(\sigma_{k}-1)\sum_{\iota\neq i}\left[\frac{\hat{r}_{i\iota,k}r_{i\iota,k}}{\hat{r}_{ji,k}r_{ji,k}}\hat{\lambda}_{j\iota,k}\lambda_{j\iota,k}\right]\frac{\hat{\rho}_{i,k}\rho_{i,k}\hat{w}_{i}w_{i}L_{i}}{\hat{\rho}_{j,k}\rho_{j,k}\hat{w}_{j}w_{j}L_{j}}$
\end_inset

 accounts for cross-demand effects in foreign markets—see Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: XSE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 from Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-Structure-of-Optimal-Policy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
[\textrm{optimal import tax}]\ \ \ \  & 1+t_{ji,k}^{*}=\frac{-\frac{\mu_{k}}{1+\mu_{k}}\hat{r}_{ji,k}r_{ji,k}\text{\ensuremath{\Phi}}_{ji,k}^{*}}{1-\frac{\mu_{k}}{1+\mu_{k}}\sum_{\iota\neq i}\left(\hat{r}_{j\iota,k}r_{j\iota,k}\left[1+(\sigma_{k}-1)(1-\hat{\lambda}_{j\iota,k}\lambda_{j\iota,k})\right]\right)}\nonumber \\{}
[\textrm{optimal export subsidy}]\ \ \ \  & 1+x_{ij,k}^{*}=\frac{(\sigma_{k}-1)\sum_{n\neq i}\left[(1+t_{ni,g}^{*})\hat{\lambda}_{nj,k}\lambda_{nj,k}\right]}{1+(\sigma_{k}-1)(1-\hat{\lambda}_{ij,k}\lambda_{ij,k})},\nonumber \\{}
[\textrm{change in taxes}]\ \ \ \  & \widehat{1+s_{i,k}}=\frac{1+\mu_{k}}{1+s_{i,k}};\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \widehat{1+t_{ji,k}}=\frac{1+t_{ji,k}^{*}}{1+t_{ji,k}};\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \widehat{1+x_{ij,k}}=\frac{1+x_{ij,k}^{*}}{1+x_{ij,k}}.\label{eq: hat-algebra (1)}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Since the rest of the world is passive in their use of taxes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{1+s_{n,k}}=\widehat{1+t_{jn,k}}=\widehat{1+x_{nj,k}}=1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n\neq i$
\end_inset

.
 To determine the change in expenditure shares, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

, we need to determine the change in consumer price indexes.
 Invoking the CES structure of within-industry demand, we can express the
 change in 
\emph on
market 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

–industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset


\emph default
's consumer price index as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
[\textrm{price indexes}]\ \ \ \ \ \  & \hat{\tilde{P}}_{i,k}=\sum_{n\in\mathbb{C}}\left(\lambda_{ni,k}\left[\frac{\widehat{1+t_{ni,k}}}{(\widehat{1+x_{ni,k}})(\widehat{1+s_{n,k}})}\widehat{w}_{n}\hat{\rho}_{n,k}^{-\mu_{k}}\right]^{1-\sigma_{k}}\right)^{\frac{1}{1-\sigma_{k}}}.\label{eq: hat-algebra (2)}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{n,k}=L_{n,k}/L_{n}$
\end_inset

 denotes industry 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

's sales share in origin 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

, which—under free entry—is equal to the share of origin 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

's workers employed in that industry.
 The above formulation uses the fact that, by free entry, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{M}_{i,k}=\hat{\rho}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

.
 Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tilde{P}}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

, we can calculate the change in expenditure and revenue shares as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
[\textrm{expenditure shares}]\ \ \ \ \ \  & \hat{\lambda}_{ji,k}=\left[\frac{\widehat{1+t_{ji,k}}}{(\widehat{1+x_{ji,k}})(\widehat{1+s_{j,k}})}\widehat{w}_{j}\hat{\rho}_{j,k}^{-\mu_{k}}\right]^{1-\sigma_{k}}\hat{\tilde{P}}_{i,k}^{\sigma_{k}-1}\nonumber \\{}
[\textrm{revenue shares}]\ \ \ \ \  & \hat{r}_{ji,k}=\left(\frac{\widehat{1+x_{ji,k}}}{\widehat{1+t_{ji,k}}}\hat{\lambda}_{ji,k}\hat{Y}_{i}\right)\left(\sum_{n\in\mathbb{C}}\frac{\widehat{1+x_{jn,k}}}{\widehat{1+t_{jn,k}}}\hat{\lambda}_{jn,k}\hat{Y}_{n}\right)^{-1}.\label{eq: hat-algebra (3)}
\end{align}

\end_inset

The change in the wage rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and industry-level sales shares, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

, are dictated by the labor market clearing (LMC) condition, which ensures
 that industry-level sales match wage payments, industry by industry:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
[\textrm{LMC}]\ \ \ \ \ \ \hat{\rho}_{i,k}\rho_{i,k}\hat{w}_{i}w_{i}L_{i}=\sum_{j\in\mathbb{C}}\left[\frac{(1+x_{ij,k}^{*})(1+s_{i,k}^{*})}{1+t_{ij,k}^{*}}\hat{\lambda}_{ij,k}\lambda_{ij,k}e_{j,k}\hat{Y}_{j}Y_{j}\right];\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\hat{\rho}_{i,k}\rho_{i,k}=1.\label{eq: hat-algebra (4)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The change in national expenditure, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{Y}_{i}$
\end_inset

, is governed by the balanced budget (BB) condition, which ensures that
 total expenditure matches total income from wage payments and tax revenues:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
[\textrm{BB}]\ \ \ \ \hat{Y}_{i}Y_{i}= & \hat{w}_{i}w_{i}L_{i}-\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\left[s_{i,k}^{*}\hat{\lambda}_{ii,k}\lambda_{ii,k}e_{i,k}\hat{Y}_{i}Y_{i}\right]\nonumber \\
+ & \sum_{j\neq i}\sum_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\left(\frac{t_{ji,k}^{*}}{1+t_{ji,k}^{*}}\lambda_{ji,k}\hat{\lambda}_{ji,k}e_{i,k}\hat{Y}_{i}Y_{i}+\frac{1-(1+x_{ij,k}^{*})(1+s_{i,k}^{*})}{1+t_{ij,k}^{*}}\lambda_{ij,k}\hat{\lambda}_{ij,k}e_{j,k}\hat{Y}_{j}Y_{j}\right).\label{eq: hat-algebra (5)}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: hat-algebra (1)"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: hat-algebra (5)"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 represent a system of 
\begin_inset Formula $2N+NK+\left(2(N-1)+1\right)K$
\end_inset

 independent equations and unknowns.
 The independent unknown variables are 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 unknowns), 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{Y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 unknowns), 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\rho}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $NK$
\end_inset

 unknowns), 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{1+s_{i,k}}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 unknowns), 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{1+t_{ji,k}}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $(N-1)K$
\end_inset

 unknowns), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{1+x_{ij,k}}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $(N-1)K$
\end_inset

 unknowns).
 Solving the aforementioned system is possible with information on observable
 data points, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}$
\end_inset

, and estimated parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta\equiv\left\{ \mu_{k},\sigma_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Once we solve this system, the welfare consequences of country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's optimal policy are automatically determined.
 The following proposition outlines this result.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Under Proposition 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "prop Hat-Algebra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the optimal policy specification uses our approximation for 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

.
 In online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Exact Quantitative Analysis"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, we examine the accuracy of our approximation and outline how our optimization-
free approach can be alternatively conducted with an exact formula for 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{ji,k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "prop Hat-Algebra"

\end_inset

Suppose we have data on observable shares, national accounts, and applied
 taxes, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}=\left\{ \lambda_{ji,k},r_{ji,k},e_{i,k},\text{\ensuremath{\rho}}_{i,k},Y_{i},w_{i}L_{i},x_{ij,k},t_{ji,k},s_{i,k}\right\} _{j,i,k}$
\end_inset

, and information on structural parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta\equiv\left\{ \mu_{k},\sigma_{k}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 We can determine the economic consequences of country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's optimal policy by calculating 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{X}=\left\{ \hat{Y}_{i},\hat{w}_{i},\hat{\rho}_{i,k},\widehat{1+s_{i,k}},\widehat{1+t_{ji,k}},\widehat{1+x_{ij,k}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 as the solution to the system of Equations 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: hat-algebra (1)"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

-
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: hat-algebra (5)"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 After solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{X}$
\end_inset

, we can fully determine the welfare consequence of country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's optimal policy as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{W}_{i}=\hat{Y}_{i}/\prod_{k\in\mathbb{K}}\hat{\tilde{P}}_{i,k}^{e_{i,k}},\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ (\forall n\in\mathbb{C})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\tilde{P}}_{i,k}$
\end_inset

 is determined by Equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq: hat-algebra (2)"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{X}$
\end_inset

 and data, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{D}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To take stock, the optimization-free procedure described by Proposition
 1 simplifies the task of computing the gains from 
\emph on
first-best
\emph default
 trade and industrial policies.
 We can use a similar procedure (based on Theorems 2 and 3) to compute the
 gains from second-best trade policies—see online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Quantitative-Methodology FE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 Without Proposition 1, we would have to rely on numerical optimization
 to recover country 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's optimal policy.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Such a problem is typically formulated as a Mathematical Programming with
 Equilibrium Constraints (MPEC) problem–see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ossa2014trade"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for further details.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 As noted earlier, numerical optimization can become increasingly difficult-to-i
mplement when dealing with many free-moving policy variables.
 Furthermore, in many instances, obtaining credible results from numerical
 optimization requires specialized commercial solvers like 
\noun on
Snopt
\noun default
 or 
\noun on
Knitro
\noun default
.
 Propositions 1's optimization-free procedure allows us to bypass such complicat
ions, delivering notable gains in computational speed and accuracy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Consequences of Non-Cooperative Policies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our first set of results elucidates policy consequences when governments
 act non-cooperatively.
 These results convey two basic points.
 First, non-cooperative trade taxes are ineffective at correcting misallocation
 in domestic industries—even without retaliation.
 Second, the cost of retaliation is sizable.
 Together these results point to little justification for non-cooperative
 trade taxation even in 2nd-best economies plagued with misallocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab: computed gains"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 reports the gains from optimal non-cooperative policies under free and
 restricted entry.
 The first three columns in each case report welfare gains assuming the
 rest of the world does not retaliate.
 The fourth column reports net welfare effects after retaliation.
 The 1st-best non-cooperative policy consists of Pigouvian subsidies, import
 tariffs, and export subsidies (Theorem 1); the 2nd-best consists of only
 import tariffs and export subsidies (Theorem 2); the 3rd-best consists
 of only import tariffs (Theorem 3).
\end_layout

