\begin_layout Standard
We can draw two main conclusions from Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab: computed gains"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 First, trade taxes are a poor 2nd-best substitute for Pigouvian subsidies.
 Trade taxes can replicate only 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

 of the welfare gains attainable under the 1st-best policy that combines
 Pigouvian subsidies with trade taxes.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
This finding echoes the numerical result in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "balistreri2009sub"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that optimal tariffs yield smaller gains in the presence of positive firm-level
 markups.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Under free entry, the 1st-best non-cooperative policy increases welfare
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $3.05$
\end_inset

% on average, whereas 2nd-best trade taxes/subsidies raise welfare by only
 
\begin_inset Formula $1.19$
\end_inset

%.
 3rd-best import tariffs (not paired with export subsidies) are half as
 effective.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These results reflect the tension between the terms-of-trade and allocate
 efficiency emphasized in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 Since our estimated scale and trade elasticities satisfy 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})<0$
\end_inset

, correcting inter-industry misallocation with trade policy worsens the
 terms-of-trade—making it difficult for 2nd-best trade policies to strike
 a balance between these two policy targets.
 We elucidate this point further in online Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Appendix Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 by artificially raising 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})$
\end_inset

 and recomputing the gains from policy.
 The results displayed in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig: Tension 2nd-Best Trade Taxes (Appendix)  "
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 of the online appendix point to a sharp rise in the efficacy of 2nd-best
 trade taxes as 
\begin_inset Formula $Cov(\sigma_{k},\mu_{k})$
\end_inset

 is artificially inflated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Second, we find that retaliation more than wipes out the gains from non-cooperat
ive taxation.
 The net effect of non-cooperative policies 
\emph on
after retaliation
\emph default
 is a welfare loss of 
\begin_inset Formula $1.20$
\end_inset

% under free entry.
 Retaliation, in our calculation, occurs through the reciprocal adoption
 of optimal trade taxes by trading partners.
 As noted in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec: Tension"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

, the cost of retaliation may not deter a short-sighted government from
 erecting trade taxes—at least when trade taxes are a less politically-controver
sial instrument for correcting misallocation.
 In such cases, our finding that trade policy is incapable of improving
 misallocation should serve as a deterrent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The 
\size large
Gains from International Cooperation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose governments recognize the danger of non-cooperation and limit themselves
 to the cooperative policy outlined in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-Structure-of-Optimal-Policy"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
 As we show next, cooperative countries risk 
\emph on
immiserizing growth 
\emph default
if they take the lead in policy implementation.
 This issue can cause a race to the bottom in industrial policy implementation
 but can be resolved via a deep agreement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Immiserizing Effects of 
\emph on
Unilateral
\emph default
 Industrial Policy Implementation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose countries limit themselves to the efficient policy consisting of
 zero trade taxes and scale-correcting industrial policies.
 Unilateral industrial policy implementation, under this arrangement, worsens
 the ToT and can lead to immiserizing growth (Conjecture 2).
 In this section, we show that these immiserizing welfare effects do, in
 fact, occur in most countries.
\end_layout

